scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1977-4-1,commonlii-LKCA-1977-4,80 NLR 256,LKCA,1977,A misdescription of land boundaries in a deed does not invalidate the deed.,"That as the evidence and the findings of the Trial Judge show that the assessment numbers given in the respective deeds are the correct assessment numbers of the allotment of land each party intended to buy and in fact purchased and was in possession of, the description of the land by the boundaries was a misdescription which although it would not invalidate the deeds, could be rejected or ignored. The maxim falsa demonstrate non nocet which is embodied in section 95 of the Evidence Ordinance applied. Per Pathirana, J. ""The Assessment numbers provide a definite and sufficient description of the land in the respective deeds of the plaintiff and the defendant of what was intendend to pass and the descriptions in those deeds by boundaries are erroneous"". A PPEAL from the judgment of the District Court of Kandy. H. W. Jayewardene with B, J. Fernando and Miss S. Senaratne for Defendant-Appellant. T. B. Dissanayake for Plaintiff-Respondent",SRC029-s95,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1984-24-1,commonlii-LKCA-1984-24,(1985) 2 Sri LR 35,LKCA,1984,A party to a suit cannot set up the title of a third party who is not their predecessor in title and who has not been joined in the action.,A party to a suit cannot under s. 3 of the Prescription Ordinance set up the title of a third party who is not his predecessor in title and who has not been joined in the action. The judgment in a case must be declaratory of the right of a party to the suit not of a stranger,SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1986-39-1,commonlii-LKCA-1986-39,(1986) 1 Sri LR 378,LKCA,1986,"In a vindicatory suit, the plaintiff must prove their own title.","In a vindicatory suit the plaintiff must prove his title. Having failed to prove his own title, he cannot rely on the weakness of the 1st defendant's title. Whatever the strength of the 1st defendant's case, if the plaintiff fails to establish his title, plaintiff's case must necessarily fail",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1988-51-1,commonlii-LKCA-1988-51,(1988) 2 Sri LR 264,LKCA,1988,A declaration of title cannot be sought on different media than those on which the title was originally based.,"(1) The Media on which the plaintiff now seeks a declaration of title are different from. the media on which he, based his title in the earlier case: The cause of action is different. (2) Prescriptive title has to be re-considered on the basis of the new character in which the plaintiff figures in the second action. Plaintiff would have passing to him whatever title by prescription which Jefferjee had and this differs from the prescriptive title he claimed in the earlier case",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1988-51-2,commonlii-LKCA-1988-51,(1988) 2 Sri LR 264,LKCA,1988,A prescriptive title must be re-considered based on the new character in which the plaintiff figures in a subsequent action.,"(1) The Media on which the plaintiff now seeks a declaration of title are different from. the media on which he, based his title in the earlier case: The cause of action is different. (2) Prescriptive title has to be re-considered on the basis of the new character in which the plaintiff figures in the second action. Plaintiff would have passing to him whatever title by prescription which Jefferjee had and this differs from the prescriptive title he claimed in the earlier case",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1998-51-1,commonlii-LKCA-1998-51,(1998) 3 Sri LR 46,LKCA,1998,A contract for a building lease ends if the building is burnt down without the fault of the landlord or tenant.,"1. Where a building which is the subject matter is burnt down without the fault of the landlord or tenant, the contract is at an end. 2. The trial Judge on a preponderance of evidence led came to a finding that the plaintiff-appellant had set fire to the house. This is a finding of primary fact by a trial Judge who had seen and heard the witness based upon the credibility of such witness, which is entitled to great weight and utmost consideration. 3. The order of the Rent Board was based on the premise that there existed a building which could be repaired. In the case of a house being let if that is completely burnt, the lease comes to an end, but not where the tenant is able to exercise, many of his rights under the lease notwithstanding the complete destruction of the buildings",SRC029-s145(1),none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1999-83-1,commonlii-LKCA-1999-83,(2000) 3 Sri LR 23,LKCA,1999,"In a usufructuary mortgage, prescription can only run from a breach of the condition related to the use and enjoyment of the property.",(1) A usufructuary mortgage being a devise for use and enjoyment of property in lieu of interest - prescription could run only from a breach of that condition. (2) Person who entered property in a subordinate character cannot claim prescriptive rights till he changes his character by an overt act. The proof of adverse possession is a condition precedent to the claim for prescriptive rights. (3) Usufructuary mortgagee is bound to the terms of the Bond itself and his rights are confined to the terms in the bond. He cannot claim prescriptive rights till he changes his character and claim adversely disregarding the conditions of the Bond. There is no question of merger of the entirety of the rights and no prescriptive rights being acquired by the Mortgagee by adverse possession,SRC071-s5,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1999-83-2,commonlii-LKCA-1999-83,(2000) 3 Sri LR 23,LKCA,1999,A person who enters a property in a subordinate character cannot claim prescriptive rights until they change their character by an overt act.,(1) A usufructuary mortgage being a devise for use and enjoyment of property in lieu of interest - prescription could run only from a breach of that condition. (2) Person who entered property in a subordinate character cannot claim prescriptive rights till he changes his character by an overt act. The proof of adverse possession is a condition precedent to the claim for prescriptive rights. (3) Usufructuary mortgagee is bound to the terms of the Bond itself and his rights are confined to the terms in the bond. He cannot claim prescriptive rights till he changes his character and claim adversely disregarding the conditions of the Bond. There is no question of merger of the entirety of the rights and no prescriptive rights being acquired by the Mortgagee by adverse possession,SRC071-s5,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1999-83-3,commonlii-LKCA-1999-83,(2000) 3 Sri LR 23,LKCA,1999,A usufructuary mortgagee is bound by the terms of the bond and their rights are limited to those terms.,(1) A usufructuary mortgage being a devise for use and enjoyment of property in lieu of interest - prescription could run only from a breach of that condition. (2) Person who entered property in a subordinate character cannot claim prescriptive rights till he changes his character by an overt act. The proof of adverse possession is a condition precedent to the claim for prescriptive rights. (3) Usufructuary mortgagee is bound to the terms of the Bond itself and his rights are confined to the terms in the bond. He cannot claim prescriptive rights till he changes his character and claim adversely disregarding the conditions of the Bond. There is no question of merger of the entirety of the rights and no prescriptive rights being acquired by the Mortgagee by adverse possession,SRC071-s5,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2001-1-1,commonlii-LKCA-2001-1,(2002) 2 Sri LR 384,LKCA,2001,A party's prescription of land is determined by the land's physical boundaries as seen from the land itself.,"The appellant has prescribed to the land as seen from - (1) Continued possession of the corpus after the death of E and from 1969 after the widow left the corpus. (2) Planting with coconuts, etc., and enjoying the produce. (3) Friends and relations arranging a marriage in 1970, with the marriage ceremony being taken in the house and premises in suit. (4) The widow of E not attending the marriage ceremony. (5) Building extensions, effecting repairs, renting a portion of the house in 1970. 385 (6) Continued possession after marriage. (7) Payment of rates and taxes from 1973. (8) Long and undisturbed possession of the corpus been corroborated by the evidence of the Grama Sevaka",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2001-1-2,commonlii-LKCA-2001-1,(2002) 2 Sri LR 384,LKCA,2001,Continued possession of the corpus after the death of E and from 1969 after the widow left the corpus is relevant.,"The appellant has prescribed to the land as seen from - (1) Continued possession of the corpus after the death of E and from 1969 after the widow left the corpus. (2) Planting with coconuts, etc., and enjoying the produce. (3) Friends and relations arranging a marriage in 1970, with the marriage ceremony being taken in the house and premises in suit. (4) The widow of E not attending the marriage ceremony. (5) Building extensions, effecting repairs, renting a portion of the house in 1970. 385 (6) Continued possession after marriage. (7) Payment of rates and taxes from 1973. (8) Long and undisturbed possession of the corpus been corroborated by the evidence of the Grama Sevaka",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,fact-specific,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2001-1-3,commonlii-LKCA-2001-1,(2002) 2 Sri LR 384,LKCA,2001,"Planting with coconuts, etc., and enjoying the produce is a relevant activity.","The appellant has prescribed to the land as seen from - (1) Continued possession of the corpus after the death of E and from 1969 after the widow left the corpus. (2) Planting with coconuts, etc., and enjoying the produce. (3) Friends and relations arranging a marriage in 1970, with the marriage ceremony being taken in the house and premises in suit. (4) The widow of E not attending the marriage ceremony. (5) Building extensions, effecting repairs, renting a portion of the house in 1970. 385 (6) Continued possession after marriage. (7) Payment of rates and taxes from 1973. (8) Long and undisturbed possession of the corpus been corroborated by the evidence of the Grama Sevaka",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,fact-specific,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2001-99-1,commonlii-LKCA-2001-99,(2002) 1 Sri LR 148,LKCA,2001,The right of dominium of the plaintiffs is in contest with the declaration of adverse possession by the defendants.,"(1) The contest is between the right of dominium of the plaintiffs and the declaration of adverse possession amounting to prescription by the defendants. (2) The moment title is proved the right to possess it, is presumed. (3) Thus, even if the Court found that the defendants had prescribed to the corpus, the proper answer to the 1st issue would have been yes, but the defendants have prescribed. (4) An averment of prescription by a plaintiff in a plaint after pleading paper title is employed only to buttress his paper title. (5) For the Court to have come to its decision as to whether the plaintiff had dominium, the proving of paper title is sufficient. 149 (6) The mere fact that the plaintiff claimed both on deeds as well as by long possession did not entail the plaintiff to prove prescriptive title thereto. Their possession was presumed on proving paper title. The averment of prescription in the plaint did not cast any burden upon the plaintiff to prove a separate title by prescription in addition to paper title. (7) Once paper title became undisputed the burden shifted to the defendants to show that they had independent rights in the form of prescription as claimed by them. Per Wigneswaran, J. ""A person is in possession of a house for example, when he or his servants or licensees are living in it, if he or they are absent from it, he would still be held to be in possession, if such absence was only temporary. In this case the brother of the 1st plaintiff (1st defendant) could have been in occupation and still the 1st plaintiff would have been in possession simultaneously.""",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2001-99-2,commonlii-LKCA-2001-99,(2002) 1 Sri LR 148,LKCA,2001,The right to possess a title is presumed once it is proved.,"(1) The contest is between the right of dominium of the plaintiffs and the declaration of adverse possession amounting to prescription by the defendants. (2) The moment title is proved the right to possess it, is presumed. (3) Thus, even if the Court found that the defendants had prescribed to the corpus, the proper answer to the 1st issue would have been yes, but the defendants have prescribed. (4) An averment of prescription by a plaintiff in a plaint after pleading paper title is employed only to buttress his paper title. (5) For the Court to have come to its decision as to whether the plaintiff had dominium, the proving of paper title is sufficient. 149 (6) The mere fact that the plaintiff claimed both on deeds as well as by long possession did not entail the plaintiff to prove prescriptive title thereto. Their possession was presumed on proving paper title. The averment of prescription in the plaint did not cast any burden upon the plaintiff to prove a separate title by prescription in addition to paper title. (7) Once paper title became undisputed the burden shifted to the defendants to show that they had independent rights in the form of prescription as claimed by them. Per Wigneswaran, J. ""A person is in possession of a house for example, when he or his servants or licensees are living in it, if he or they are absent from it, he would still be held to be in possession, if such absence was only temporary. In this case the brother of the 1st plaintiff (1st defendant) could have been in occupation and still the 1st plaintiff would have been in possession simultaneously.""",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2001-99-3,commonlii-LKCA-2001-99,(2002) 1 Sri LR 148,LKCA,2001,"Even if the defendants had prescribed to the corpus, the proper answer to the 1st issue would have been yes.","(1) The contest is between the right of dominium of the plaintiffs and the declaration of adverse possession amounting to prescription by the defendants. (2) The moment title is proved the right to possess it, is presumed. (3) Thus, even if the Court found that the defendants had prescribed to the corpus, the proper answer to the 1st issue would have been yes, but the defendants have prescribed. (4) An averment of prescription by a plaintiff in a plaint after pleading paper title is employed only to buttress his paper title. (5) For the Court to have come to its decision as to whether the plaintiff had dominium, the proving of paper title is sufficient. 149 (6) The mere fact that the plaintiff claimed both on deeds as well as by long possession did not entail the plaintiff to prove prescriptive title thereto. Their possession was presumed on proving paper title. The averment of prescription in the plaint did not cast any burden upon the plaintiff to prove a separate title by prescription in addition to paper title. (7) Once paper title became undisputed the burden shifted to the defendants to show that they had independent rights in the form of prescription as claimed by them. Per Wigneswaran, J. ""A person is in possession of a house for example, when he or his servants or licensees are living in it, if he or they are absent from it, he would still be held to be in possession, if such absence was only temporary. In this case the brother of the 1st plaintiff (1st defendant) could have been in occupation and still the 1st plaintiff would have been in possession simultaneously.""",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2002-76-1,commonlii-LKCA-2002-76,(2002) 3 Sri LR 201,LKCA,2002,A tenant under the Rent Act is entitled to seek statutory protection.,(1) The defendant-appellant being a tenant under the plaintiff is entitled to seek protection under the provisions of the Rent Act. Such statutory protection comes to an end only upon (a) by handing back of the premises to the landlord or (b) by order of a competent court. It is open to a tenant to recall a promise to surrender possession. (2) The common law rights of a landlord to institute action for an order of ejectment of a tenant is curtailed by the provisions of the Rent Act. (3) A conditional agreement cannot form the basis for an order of ejectment. A PPEAL from the judgment of the District Court of Mt. Lavinia. 202,SRC001-s2,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2002-76-2,commonlii-LKCA-2002-76,(2002) 3 Sri LR 201,LKCA,2002,The common law rights of a landlord to institute action for an order of ejectment of a tenant are curtailed.,(1) The defendant-appellant being a tenant under the plaintiff is entitled to seek protection under the provisions of the Rent Act. Such statutory protection comes to an end only upon (a) by handing back of the premises to the landlord or (b) by order of a competent court. It is open to a tenant to recall a promise to surrender possession. (2) The common law rights of a landlord to institute action for an order of ejectment of a tenant is curtailed by the provisions of the Rent Act. (3) A conditional agreement cannot form the basis for an order of ejectment. A PPEAL from the judgment of the District Court of Mt. Lavinia. 202,SRC001-s2,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2002-76-3,commonlii-LKCA-2002-76,(2002) 3 Sri LR 201,LKCA,2002,A conditional agreement cannot be used as the basis for an order of ejectment.,(1) The defendant-appellant being a tenant under the plaintiff is entitled to seek protection under the provisions of the Rent Act. Such statutory protection comes to an end only upon (a) by handing back of the premises to the landlord or (b) by order of a competent court. It is open to a tenant to recall a promise to surrender possession. (2) The common law rights of a landlord to institute action for an order of ejectment of a tenant is curtailed by the provisions of the Rent Act. (3) A conditional agreement cannot form the basis for an order of ejectment. A PPEAL from the judgment of the District Court of Mt. Lavinia. 202,SRC001-s2,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2003-66-1,commonlii-LKCA-2003-66,(2003) 2 Sri LR 341,LKCA,2003,Privity of contract is the foundation of the right to relief in an action by a lessor against an overholding lessee.,"(i) Privity of contract is the foundation of the right to relief in an action by a lessor against an over holding lessee for restoration and ejectment and issues as to title are irrelevant. A lessee who has entered into occupation is precluded from disputing his lessor's title until he has first restored the property in fulfillment of his contractual obligations. (ii) The plaintiff who claims qua landlord to eject his tenant in occupation, must establish that privity of contract between himself and the tenant exists at the relevant date, and if it does exist the tenant is precluded by provisions of section 116 of the Evidence Ordinance from disputing the plaintiff's title to the premises",SRC029-s116,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2003-66-2,commonlii-LKCA-2003-66,(2003) 2 Sri LR 341,LKCA,2003,A plaintiff claiming to be a landlord must establish the existence of privity of contract with the tenant at the relevant date.,"(i) Privity of contract is the foundation of the right to relief in an action by a lessor against an over holding lessee for restoration and ejectment and issues as to title are irrelevant. A lessee who has entered into occupation is precluded from disputing his lessor's title until he has first restored the property in fulfillment of his contractual obligations. (ii) The plaintiff who claims qua landlord to eject his tenant in occupation, must establish that privity of contract between himself and the tenant exists at the relevant date, and if it does exist the tenant is precluded by provisions of section 116 of the Evidence Ordinance from disputing the plaintiff's title to the premises",SRC029-s116,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2007-3-1,commonlii-LKCA-2007-3,(2007) 1 Sri LR 13,LKCA,2007,"A party asserting fraud does not have to prove the fact, but rather the burden is on the other party to dispel all suspicion.",(1) Every circumstance that is to be considered by Court need not be raised by way of an issue. The real issue that the petitioner was required to meet was whether the impugned last will was a forgery. The allegation of fraud with regard to a last will is an instance which finds an exception to the rule of evidence that the party asserting (fraud) has to prove the fact. The Rule set up by Courts through the line of authorities is that when there is a suggestion of fraud even if the evidence is not of such a nature as to justify a finding of fraud the burden is cast on the propounder of the Will to dispel all suspicion if he were to have the will proved and probate granted. (2) In considering the judgment as a whole and not part by part it is clear that the trial judge did not reject and dismiss the,SRC029-s101,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2007-3-2,commonlii-LKCA-2007-3,(2007) 1 Sri LR 13,LKCA,2007,A judgment should be considered as a whole rather than part by part.,(1) Every circumstance that is to be considered by Court need not be raised by way of an issue. The real issue that the petitioner was required to meet was whether the impugned last will was a forgery. The allegation of fraud with regard to a last will is an instance which finds an exception to the rule of evidence that the party asserting (fraud) has to prove the fact. The Rule set up by Courts through the line of authorities is that when there is a suggestion of fraud even if the evidence is not of such a nature as to justify a finding of fraud the burden is cast on the propounder of the Will to dispel all suspicion if he were to have the will proved and probate granted. (2) In considering the judgment as a whole and not part by part it is clear that the trial judge did not reject and dismiss the,SRC029-s101,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1979-5-1,commonlii-LKSC-1979-5,(1995) 1 Sri LR 7,LKSC,1979,A person who holds themselves out as an agent of another may be presumed to be a sufficient medium for receiving notice on behalf of the principal.,"(1) The 1st defendant had held himself out as the agent of the other defendants and the other defendants by their own conduct held out that the 1st defendant was their sole spokesman in relation to the transaction so much as to induce Gnanam, Managing Director of the plaintiff company and the lawyers for the company that the 1st defendant was a sufficient medium through which notice could reach the other defendants. The 1st defendant did not give evidence and the court is entitled to draw the presumption that had he given evidence; such evidence would have been unfavourable to the case of the defendants - see section 114 illustration (f) of the Evidence Ordinance. (2) The draft deed of transfer was received by 1st defendant four days prior to the date fixed for signing the transfer. There was no uncertainty in relation to the vendors and vendee, the subject matter of the sale and the consideration. There was sufficient compliance on the part of the plaintiff in regard to the stipulation, of notice of the, draft. Although a plaintiff who breaks-an-essential term of the agreement cannot claim specific performance, trivial breaches do not disentitle a party from claiming relief. (3) Time was of the essence of the contract only in respect of the last date for signing the deed of transfer and in no other respect On the last date for signing, the defendants were not available and wanted a further date to sign the deed of transfer. 8 (4) The plaintiff was entitled to a decree for specific performance",SRC029-s114,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1979-5-2,commonlii-LKSC-1979-5,(1995) 1 Sri LR 7,LKSC,1979,A party who breaks an essential term of an agreement cannot claim specific performance.,"(1) The 1st defendant had held himself out as the agent of the other defendants and the other defendants by their own conduct held out that the 1st defendant was their sole spokesman in relation to the transaction so much as to induce Gnanam, Managing Director of the plaintiff company and the lawyers for the company that the 1st defendant was a sufficient medium through which notice could reach the other defendants. The 1st defendant did not give evidence and the court is entitled to draw the presumption that had he given evidence; such evidence would have been unfavourable to the case of the defendants - see section 114 illustration (f) of the Evidence Ordinance. (2) The draft deed of transfer was received by 1st defendant four days prior to the date fixed for signing the transfer. There was no uncertainty in relation to the vendors and vendee, the subject matter of the sale and the consideration. There was sufficient compliance on the part of the plaintiff in regard to the stipulation, of notice of the, draft. Although a plaintiff who breaks-an-essential term of the agreement cannot claim specific performance, trivial breaches do not disentitle a party from claiming relief. (3) Time was of the essence of the contract only in respect of the last date for signing the deed of transfer and in no other respect On the last date for signing, the defendants were not available and wanted a further date to sign the deed of transfer. 8 (4) The plaintiff was entitled to a decree for specific performance",SRC029-s114,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1979-5-3,commonlii-LKSC-1979-5,(1995) 1 Sri LR 7,LKSC,1979,Time is of the essence of a contract only in respect of specific dates agreed upon by the parties.,"(1) The 1st defendant had held himself out as the agent of the other defendants and the other defendants by their own conduct held out that the 1st defendant was their sole spokesman in relation to the transaction so much as to induce Gnanam, Managing Director of the plaintiff company and the lawyers for the company that the 1st defendant was a sufficient medium through which notice could reach the other defendants. The 1st defendant did not give evidence and the court is entitled to draw the presumption that had he given evidence; such evidence would have been unfavourable to the case of the defendants - see section 114 illustration (f) of the Evidence Ordinance. (2) The draft deed of transfer was received by 1st defendant four days prior to the date fixed for signing the transfer. There was no uncertainty in relation to the vendors and vendee, the subject matter of the sale and the consideration. There was sufficient compliance on the part of the plaintiff in regard to the stipulation, of notice of the, draft. Although a plaintiff who breaks-an-essential term of the agreement cannot claim specific performance, trivial breaches do not disentitle a party from claiming relief. (3) Time was of the essence of the contract only in respect of the last date for signing the deed of transfer and in no other respect On the last date for signing, the defendants were not available and wanted a further date to sign the deed of transfer. 8 (4) The plaintiff was entitled to a decree for specific performance",SRC029-s114,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1993-49-1,commonlii-LKSC-1993-49,(1994) 3 Sri LR 245,LKSC,1993,The amalgamation of a lot with another land is possible.,"(1) Amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda was possible and the corpus (Batadombagahawatta) did not include lots 5B, 5C, 5D and 6B and should be excluded. (2) The causes of the 3rd defendant and 4th defendant had to be considered separately. The deeds of the 3rd defendant referred to undivided interests in the whole land, while the deed by which' Ratnayake's widow conveyed her interests to the 4th defendant referred to interests in a divided lot. (3) The amicable partition on Plan 1053 with the amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda in 1951, significant improvement of lot 5 by planting the entirety of lot 5 (except the paddy portion and a narrow slip adjoining the main road) and the 4th defendant's dealing with lot 5 as a distinct entity along with the continuous exclusive, undisturbed, adverse and uninterrupted possession by herself and her predecessors in title prove her claim to have prescribed to lot 5. (4) The partition should be confined to lots 3, 4 and 7 as shown in the preliminary plan but 4th defendant will not be entitled to any shares. (5) The interests of Tudor, one of the three children of the 3rd defendant, will remain unallotted if the 3rd defendant, on being given an opportunity, fails to prove that these rights passed to her",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1993-49-2,commonlii-LKSC-1993-49,(1994) 3 Sri LR 245,LKSC,1993,The causes of separate defendants must be considered separately.,"(1) Amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda was possible and the corpus (Batadombagahawatta) did not include lots 5B, 5C, 5D and 6B and should be excluded. (2) The causes of the 3rd defendant and 4th defendant had to be considered separately. The deeds of the 3rd defendant referred to undivided interests in the whole land, while the deed by which' Ratnayake's widow conveyed her interests to the 4th defendant referred to interests in a divided lot. (3) The amicable partition on Plan 1053 with the amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda in 1951, significant improvement of lot 5 by planting the entirety of lot 5 (except the paddy portion and a narrow slip adjoining the main road) and the 4th defendant's dealing with lot 5 as a distinct entity along with the continuous exclusive, undisturbed, adverse and uninterrupted possession by herself and her predecessors in title prove her claim to have prescribed to lot 5. (4) The partition should be confined to lots 3, 4 and 7 as shown in the preliminary plan but 4th defendant will not be entitled to any shares. (5) The interests of Tudor, one of the three children of the 3rd defendant, will remain unallotted if the 3rd defendant, on being given an opportunity, fails to prove that these rights passed to her",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1993-49-3,commonlii-LKSC-1993-49,(1994) 3 Sri LR 245,LKSC,1993,"Exclusive, undisturbed, adverse, and uninterrupted possession of a property for a significant period can prove a claim to have prescribed to that property.","(1) Amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda was possible and the corpus (Batadombagahawatta) did not include lots 5B, 5C, 5D and 6B and should be excluded. (2) The causes of the 3rd defendant and 4th defendant had to be considered separately. The deeds of the 3rd defendant referred to undivided interests in the whole land, while the deed by which' Ratnayake's widow conveyed her interests to the 4th defendant referred to interests in a divided lot. (3) The amicable partition on Plan 1053 with the amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda in 1951, significant improvement of lot 5 by planting the entirety of lot 5 (except the paddy portion and a narrow slip adjoining the main road) and the 4th defendant's dealing with lot 5 as a distinct entity along with the continuous exclusive, undisturbed, adverse and uninterrupted possession by herself and her predecessors in title prove her claim to have prescribed to lot 5. (4) The partition should be confined to lots 3, 4 and 7 as shown in the preliminary plan but 4th defendant will not be entitled to any shares. (5) The interests of Tudor, one of the three children of the 3rd defendant, will remain unallotted if the 3rd defendant, on being given an opportunity, fails to prove that these rights passed to her",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1999-54-1,commonlii-LKSC-1999-54,(1999) 2 Sri LR 4,LKSC,1999,A non-notarial document is of no force or avail in law under section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance.,"P2 being a non-notarial document was of no force or avail in law in view of section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance. However, in a case where fraud is pleaded, put in issue and is established by the evidence on record, it is open to the court to take into consideration such document. Per G. P. S. de Silva, CJ. ''The rigour of the provisions of section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance may, on proof of fraud as in the present case, be relaxed on the principle that 'the Statute of Frauds may not be made an instrument of fraud."" 5",SRC001-s2,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-2004-21-1,commonlii-LKSC-2004-21,(2005) 1 Sri LR 31,LKSC,2004,A defendant must establish prescriptive title to the land as required by section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,1. The defendant failed to establish prescriptive title to the land as required by section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance. 2. The Court of,SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-1095-99-f.pdf-1,courts-coa-1095-99-f.pdf,,COA,,The burden of proving prescriptive title falls on the defendant where the legal title is in the plaintiff.,"It is settled law that where the legal title is in the plaintiff the burden of proving prescriptive title falls on the defendant (Siyanaris Vs Jayasinghe Udeni De Silva 52 NLR 289, Juliana Hamine Vs. Don Thomas 59 NLR 546, Sirajudeen and two others Vs. Abbas 1994 (2) SLR 365).",SRC071-s3,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-13.-ca-phc-209_17-judgment.pdf-1,courts-coa-13.-ca-phc-209_17-judgment.pdf,,COA,,"A Magistrate cannot assume jurisdiction to hear and determine a case under Section 66 of the Primary Courts' Procedure Act if a civil action is pending in a District Court, unless there is a breach of peace.","If the mere institution of a suit in a civil Court is sufficient to divest the Magistrate of his jurisdiction, the whole purpose of Section 62 will be defeated. A scheming party will be enabled to play hide and seek",SRC001-s44,section-not-found,Section 62 of the Administration of Justice Law,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dc-135-1999f-1.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dc-135-1999f-1.pdf,,COA,1999,A party invoking the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat an adverse claimant to immovable property must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"Where a party invokes the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dc-135-1999f.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dc-135-1999f.pdf,,COA,1999,A party invoking the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"Where a party invokes the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dc-326_1997.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dc-326_1997.pdf,,COA,1997,The formalities required to be observed when transferring and/or selling a land or other immovable property as laid down in Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance were not followed when executing the deed.,"In terms of Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance a sale or transfer of land has to be in writing signed by two or more witnesses before a Notary, duly attested by the Notary and the witnesses. If this is not done, the document and its contents cannot be used in evidence [Samarakaan v. Gunasekera and Another (2011) 1 SrLL.R. 149].",SRC001-s2,ok,Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a.-dc_1043_1997.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a.-dc_1043_1997.pdf,,COA,1997,"The burden of seeking and getting evidence before court, in the course of investigation of title to the land sought to be partitioned by parties before Court, prior to deciding what share should go to which party is more the duty of the judge than the contesting parties.","The burden of seeking and getting evidence before court, in the course of investigation of title to the land sought to be partitioned by parties before Court, prior to deciding what share should go to which party is more the duty of the judge than the contesting parties.",SRC029-s110,section-not-found,section 110 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-423-2000-f-22.1.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-423-2000-f-22.1.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,A person who claims prescriptive title against the rightful owner who has the paper title has a very heavy burden to prove all the requirements prescribed in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,A person who claims prescriptive title against the rightful owner who has the paper title has a very heavy burden to prove all the requirements prescribed in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,none,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-472-97-16.01.19.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-472-97-16.01.19.pdf,,COA,,"A party claiming prescriptive title must prove undisturbed and uninterrupted possession, independent or adverse to the claimant, for ten years prior to the action.","In order to initiate a prescriptive title, it is necessary to show a change in the nature of the possession and the party claiming prescriptive right should show an ouster.",SRC029-s3,section-not-found,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-PHC-126-20-Judgment-Final.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-PHC-126-20-Judgment-Final.pdf,,COA,,The pendency of civil proceedings does not constitute a legal bar to the institution or continuation of proceedings under Section 66.,"At its highest, the existence of such proceedings may be considered as surrounding circumstance, however, it does not undermine the Magistrate’s duty to inquire into the existence of a dispute affecting land and the likelihood of a breach of the peace, and to make such provisional orders as are necessary in that regard.",SRC001-s66,section-not-found,Section 66 of the Primary Courts’ Procedure Act,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-PHC-93-24-Judgment-Final.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-PHC-93-24-Judgment-Final.pdf,,COA,,"A power of attorney holder does not have locus standi to be named as a party and appear in revision proceedings under the Maintenance Act No. 37 of 1999, unless the power of attorney explicitly grants such authority.","It is a fundamental principle of the law relating to power of attorneys that the authority conferred upon an attorney must be strictly construed, and that an attorney cannot act beyond the scope of the authority expressly granted.",SRC001-s8,section-not-found,"Section 8 of the Maintenance Act, No. 37 of 1999",True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-WRT-0385-23-1.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-WRT-0385-23-1.pdf,,COA,,"The Board must consider all circumstances of the case, including the language of the notarial instrument of transfer, any difference between the sum received by the transferor and the value of the property transferred, the continuance of the transferor's possession of the property transferred, and the existence of any agreement between the transferor and the transferee, when deciding whether or not a transfer or conditional transfer is in reality a mortgage.","In any Proceedings under the Ordinance in regard to an application relating to a transfer or conditional transfer of immovable property, the Bond shall, notwithstanding anything to the contrary in section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance, or sections 91 and 92 of the Evidence Ordinance and for the purpose of deciding whether or not such transfer or conditional transfer is in reality a mortgage, take into consideration all the circumstances of the case and in particular the following matters (a) The language of the notarial instrument of transfer and where provision in regard to the right of the transferor or any other person to redeem or purchase the property transferred is contained in any other notarial instrument.",SRC001-s2,ok,section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_242_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_242_99.pdf,,COA,,"A party invoking the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights, which must be undisturbed, uninterrupted, adverse to or independent of that of the former possessor and must have lasted for at least ten years.","According to section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance such a possession must be undisturbed, uninterrupted, adverse to or independent of that of the former possessor and should have lasted for at least ten years before he could transform such possession into prescriptive title.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_476_1999.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_476_1999.pdf,,COA,1999,A person who has been dispossessed of immovable property otherwise than by process of law can institute proceedings against the person dispossessing him at any time within one year of such dispossession.,Section 4 of the Prescription Ordinance declares: ~,SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,Section 4 of the Prescription Ordinance No.22 of 1871,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_513_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_513_98.pdf,,COA,,The burden of proof is on the defendant in an action for declaration of title to property where the legal title is in the plaintiff but the property is in the possession of the defendant.,"In an action for declaration of title to property, where the legal title is in the Plaintiff but the property is in the possession of the Defendant, the burden of proof is on the Defendant - See. Siyaneris vs Jayasinghe Udenis de Silva 52 NLR 289(PC) .",SRC071-s3,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_764_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_764_99.pdf,,COA,,A lessee or licensee must first restore the property to their landlord before disputing the title of the person who gave them the lease or license.,In this case the defendant was permitted to occupy the premises with the permission of the Plaintiff. In my opinion the defendant has no defence to this action. He must give up possession to the Plaintiff.,SRC029-s116,section-not-found,section 116 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_788_97.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_788_97.pdf,,COA,,The burden of proof is on the defendant to show independent rights in the form of prescription when the plaintiff's paper title is undisputed.,Once paper title became undisputed the burden shifted to the defendants to show that they had independent rights in the form of prescription as claimed by them,SRC029-s68,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_955_1996.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_955_1996.pdf,,COA,1996,"A person who knows the value of the property is not entitled to rescission of the sale merely by reason of the fact that the price at which he has sold it, is less than half its true value.",a person who knows the value of his property is not entitled to recession of the sale merely by reason of the fact that the price at which he had sold the property is less than half its true value .....,SRC029-s101,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_badulla_1162_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_badulla_1162_99.pdf,,COA,,"A person who enters into possession of a land in one capacity is presumed to continue in possession in the same capacity, and a co-owner's possession in law is tantamount to the possession of his other co-owners.",It is not possible for him to put an end to that possession by any secret intention in his mind unless there is proof of nothing short of ouster or something equivalent to ouster.,SRC071-s3,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_balapitiya_830_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_balapitiya_830_98.pdf,,COA,,The burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on a party invoking the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property to establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"Where a party invokes the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_438_1999.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_438_1999.pdf,,COA,1999,"In an action by a lessor against an overholding lessee for restoration and ejectment, privity of contract is the foundation of the right to relief, and issues as to title are irrelevant to the proceedings.",Privity of contract (whether it be by original agreement or by attornment) is the foundation of the right to relief and issues as to title are irrelevant to the proceedings.,SRC029-s58,section-not-found,section 58 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_780_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_colombo_780_98.pdf,,COA,,"A plaintiff can claim prescriptive title to land if they have been in uninterrupted possession of the land for ten years, without paying rent or performing any service or duty, and without acknowledging the rights of another person.",This position in law is supported by many authorities. Maasdorp's Institutes (7th Edition Vol2 at 96) Wigneswaran J in Leisa and another V Simon and Another [2002 (1) SLR 148] Pathirana V Jayasundera [58 NLR 169] Dona Cecilia V Cecilia Perera and others [1987 (1) SLR 235] Wijesundara and others v Constantine Dasa and another [1987 (2) SLR 66] I will now turn to consider whether the learned District Judge correctly evaluated the evidence and applied the law relevant thereto when he accepted the prescriptive rights .I 3 of the plaintiff rejecting the defence taken up by the defendant.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_galle_123_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_galle_123_99.pdf,,COA,,A party invoking the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to defeat ownership must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"lt is trite law that where a party to an action for a declaration of title to a land invokes the Provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_homagama_1113_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_homagama_1113_98.pdf,,COA,,"A prescriptive title can be claimed if the respondent has been in possession of the land for a period of well over ten years prior to the filing of this action, adverse to the rights of the plaintiff.","In the circumstances, it is abundantly clear that the respondent had been in possession of the land claimed by the plaintiff for a period of well over ten years prior to the filing of this action on 03.05.1991, adverse to the rights of the plaintiff which is the criteria applicable to claim prescriptive title in terms of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_kuliyapitiya_293_00.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_kuliyapitiya_293_00.pdf,,COA,,A claim for recovery of property belonging to a temple is not barred by prescription under the Prescription Ordinance.,The Respondent's plea of prescription should necessarily fail.,SRC071-s34,ok-unchecked,section 34 of the Prescription Ordinance,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_rathnapura_729_00.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_rathnapura_729_00.pdf,,COA,,A possessor is always presumed to hold in his own right and as proprietor until the contrary be demonstrated.,A possessor is always presumed to hold in his own right and as proprietor until the contrary be demonstrated;,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-TAX-15-20-TAX-22-23-Judgment.pdf-1,courts-coa-TAX-15-20-TAX-22-23-Judgment.pdf,,COA,,The business of life insurance is expressly exempted from VAT.,This Court is of the view that the VAT Act itself expressly provides for the exemption of the business of life insurance from the charge of VAT.,SRC001-s2,ok,"section 25C(5)(a) of the VAT Act, PART II (b)(x)(i) of the First schedule to the VAT Act",False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-Writ-143-22.pdf-1,courts-coa-Writ-143-22.pdf,,COA,,"A Minister's discretion to requisition property under the Cooperative Societies Act should be exercised reasonably, and a failure to derequisition property after a long period of use can be considered unreasonable and subject to a Writ of Mandamus.","However, if discretion has been used in an unreasonable manner, the said decision is amenable to the Writ jurisdiction.",SRC001-s10,section-not-found,section 10(1) of the Cooperative Societies Act,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_147_2011-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_147_2011-1.pdf,,SC,2011,A person having a claim in respect of a lease at will or for a period not exceeding one month is not a necessary party to a Partition action.,"It was further held that a person having a claim in respect of a lease at will or for a period not exceeding one month, is not a necessary party to a Partition action.",SRC001-s2,ok,section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_101_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_101_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,A cause of action for damages arising from the delict of setting the law in motion is prescribed within 2 years of the arrest.,"Did the Court of Appeal fall into substantial error by completely ignoring the provisions of Section 9 of the Prescription Ordinance? Moreover, in terms of section 9 of the Prescription Ordinance, action for damages should be filed within 2 years of the arrest.",SRC071-s9,ok-unchecked,Section 9 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_102_2011-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_102_2011-1.pdf,,SC,2011,A co-owner's possession is in law the possession of his co-owners.,A co-owner’s possession is in law the possession of his co-owners. It is not possible for him to put an end to that possession by any secret intention in his mind. Nothing short of ouster or something equivalent to ouster could bring about the result.,SRC071-s3,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_104_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_104_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A party resisting restoration of possession under a partition decree must demonstrate a legally valid derivative title.,The requirement that the respondent demonstrate a legally valid derivative title ensures that the protection afforded by the proviso operates only where the respondent has a genuine claim derived from the decree holder.,SRC001-s2,ok,section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_105_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"A party who claims prescriptive title must demonstrate possession over a specifically identified corpus; failure to do so renders such a claim untenable, as a prescription cannot operate over an uncertain or unidentified land.","A person who claims a prescriptive title must demonstrate possession over a specifically identified corpus; failure to do so renders such a claim untenable, as a prescription cannot operate over an uncertain or unidentified land.",SRC029-s58,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_108_2022-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_108_2022-1.pdf,,SC,2022,"A rei vindicatio action proper requires proof of title, but an action against an overholding lessee or licensee is based on privity of contract and issues as to title are irrelevant.",What has been discussed above indicate the deference between the re vidicatio action and an acton against an overholding lessee or licensee.,SRC029-s116,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_120_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_120_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A contract by a minor is only voidable and not void, thus, the Power of Attorney remained valid until it is set aside and that subsequent setting aside of the Power of Attorney, will not affect the property rights acquired by the Petitioner as innocent third parties through the Power of Attorney.","Even if an unassisted contract is void against the minor it can be ratified and make valid by the minor after becoming a major as happened in the case at hand. Once it is ratified and made valid and used for his benefit, he is estopped from stating that it is void ab initio.",SRC071-s10,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_122_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_122_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A party cannot be expected to pay the balance of a purchase price without receiving a title deed.,There was no way to pay the balance and get title from the Plaintiffs simply because they did not have legal title to the said housing unit until the end of 2002.,SRC001-s2,ok,Sec. 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_123_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_123_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A co-owner's signature on an amicable partition plan is not sufficient to terminate co-ownership in relation to divided portions depicted in the plan.,Such placement of signature can only be construed as co-owner’s expression of consent and willingness to transfer or renounce their rights in relation to the lots allocated to the other co-owners but such plan itself cannot be construed as an instrument or writing renouncing or transferring such co-owned rights.,SRC001-s2,ok,Section 2 of the Prevention of Fraud ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_125_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_125_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,"A presumption of title arises in favour of the Respondents, and the Appellant has not succeeded in rebutting the presumption of title in favour of the Respondents.","In these circumstances, I am of the opinion that the Respondents should be restored to possession on the basis that the action filed by them was an action for declaration of title analogous to a rei vindicatio action, wherein the burden on the Respondents was to show that they held the land in dispute as the heirs of Babun Appuhamy who was a permit-holder in terms of the Land Development Ordinance, and to which they hoped to succeed.",SRC001-s2,ok,section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_128_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_128_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A prosecution under the Food Act is time barred if it is instituted more than three months after the date of detection of the offence.,"It is contended that the intention of the Legislature by imposing a time bar as contained in Section 20 (1) (b) of the Food Act for instituting an action was to ensure inter alia credibility of the Complainant, as it is with the expectation that a Complainant will initiate an action by bringing the offence to the notice of the relevant Officers at the earliest point of discovery that the offence had taken place.",SRC071-s9,none,Section 20 (1) (b) of the Food Act,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_12_2006.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_12_2006.pdf,,SC,2006,The burden of proving that there had been a change in the condition of the land solely rests on the appellants.,Therefore the burden of proving that there had been a change in the condition of the land solely rests on the appellants.,SRC029-s101,section-not-found,Section 101 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_131_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_131_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,Mere use of a footpath for the period of prescription is insufficient to establish a servitude of right of way by prescription as per section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,"If prescription is allowed to be established merely by walking through someone’s land, it would pave the way for countless claims to be levelled against owners of land. It would give rise to drastic consequences in village settings where people casually walk through the lands of each other as a practice. If all persons casually exercising physical use of footpaths across neighboring lands were to bring their claims stating that each such footpath constitutes a right of way by prescription simply by physical use, the doctrine of prescription would have far reaching and drastic consequences which were never intended by its proponents.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_132_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_132_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce or performance of service or duty or by any other act by the possessor from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred is considered adverse possession.,"The words in section 3—“A possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce or performance of service or duty or by any other act by the possessor from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred”—have been held by this Court to contain not an illustration, but a definition of “adverse possession” (see Daniel v. Markar Ram. 1843-55, 2, Vand. Rep. 44, Carrim v. Dholl 2 CLR 12).",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_134_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_134_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A party alleging that a deed is a forgery must prove the execution of the deed to establish its genuineness.,"If these deeds are excluded, the Plaintiff cannot establish his title and his action will necessarily fail.",SRC029-s114,section-not-found,section 114 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"A defendant cannot prove prescriptive title against co-owners unless the contrary is proved by cogent evidence, and possession of one co-owner is in law the possession of the other co-owners.","Unless the contrary is proved by cogent evidence, possession of one co-owner is in law the possession of the other co-owners.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The burden of proof lies on the person who wishes the court to believe in the existence of a fact.,Hence the burden of proof lies on the Appellant to prove the existence of a forgery.,SRC029-s103,section-not-found,Section 103 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"A co-owner cannot acquire prescriptive title to a co-owned property by proving exclusive possession for ten years after changing the nature of the possession to one of adverse title to the others, if the possession is not hostile to the real owner and does not amount to a denial of the owner's title.",In order to acquire prescriptive title a co-owner should prove exclusive possession for ten years after changing the nature of the possession to one of adverse title to the others.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_155_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_155_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The occupation of the premises must be of such character as is incompatible with the title of the owner.,The occupation of the premises must be of such character as is incompatible with the title of the owner.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_158_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_158_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A Notary Public who attested a Deed cannot be regarded as an attesting witness for the purposes of Section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance if he does not know the executant of the Deed.,"Therefore, the fact that, the Notary Public did not state in his Attestation that he knew the executants, leads compellingly to the conclusion that, he did not know the executants.",SRC029-s68,section-not-found,Section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_159_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_159_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,"To claim prescriptive title, a party must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights and provide documentary evidence of their adverse possession.",Where a party invokes the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property the burden of proof rests fairly and squarely on him to establish the starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights [Gratiean J in Chelliah v Wijenathan 54 N.L.R. 337 at 342].,SRC071-s3,none,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A statutory presumption in favour of the execution of a judicial or official act can be made if it is likely to have happened, considering the common course of natural events, human conduct, and public and private business.","This Court rules on a statutory presumption in favour of the execution, in terms of Section 114 (d) of the Evidence Ordinance. This Section reads as follows; 4 “The Court may presume the existence of any fact which it thinks likely to have happened, regard being had to the common course of natural events, human conduct, and public and private business in their relation to the facts of the particular case – that judicial and official acts have been regularly performed.”",SRC029-s114(d),none,Section 114 (d) of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_167_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_167_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,"A sham transaction is not a proper transfer, and the attendant circumstances can point to a constructive trust.","Precisely, if there was no intention to act upon the notarially executed document, it is no proper transfer but a sham.",SRC001-s2,ok,section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_171_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_171_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"In a possessory action, the plaintiff need not prove lawful title to the subject matter of the action.",I therefore hold that the Court of Appeal has erred when it held that the Plaintiff in order to institute this action should prove that the Plaintiff herself had lawful title.,SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,Section 4 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_172_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_172_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,"A written promise to pay a sum of money must be in writing, but no special form of writing is required, and the promise may be contained in one document or evidenced by more than one document or by an exchange of documents.","For the purpose of constituting a written promise, contract, bargain or agreement no special form of writing is required.",SRC071-s6,ok-unchecked,Section 6 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2025.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2025.pdf,,SC,2025,A deed executed before a notary can be proved by the notary if they knew the executant personally and can testify that the signature on the deed is that of the executant.,"Where a deed executed before a notary is sought to be proved, the notary can be regarded as an attesting witness within the meaning of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance if the notary knew the executant personally and can testify that the signature on the deed is that of the executant.",SRC029-s68,section-not-found,section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_191_192_2025-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_191_192_2025-1.pdf,,SC,2025,"A document marked subject to proof is admissible without formal proof unless its execution or genuineness is impeached in the pleadings and raised as an issue, or the court otherwise requires proof.","The law relating to the proof of documents has been laid down in the Civil Procedure Code (Amendment) Act, No. 17 of 2022. Section 2 of this Act introduces section 154A to be immediately inserted after section 154 of the principal enactment, which provides that when a deed or any document required by law to be attested is tendered in evidence, it shall be admissible without requiring formal proof, unless its execution or genuineness is impeached in the pleadings and raised as an issue, or the court otherwise requires proof.",SRC029-s154A,section-not-found,"Section 2 of the Civil Procedure Code (Amendment) Act, No. 17 of 2022",False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A person who possesses a land with a secret intention that he would be able to claim prescription at the end of ten years is not entitled to claim prescription under Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,"If a person, who claims prescription in terms of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance, possesses the property with a secret intention his possession cannot be considered as an adverse possession and as such he is not entitled to succeed in a claim of prescription.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_197_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_197_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"A wife cannot claim prescriptive title to a property owned by her husband, as her possession of the property is not considered adverse possession.",Further when wife possesses a property of her husband she cannot claim prescriptive title under Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance against her husband because such a possession cannot be considered to be an adverse possession against her husband.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_232_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_232_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A person who possesses an immovable property with leave and licence of the owner cannot claim prescriptive title against the owner or his heirs on the basis of the period he possessed the property.,"When a person starts possessing an immovable property with leave and licence of the owner, the presumption is that he continues to possess the immovable property on the permission originally granted and such a person or his agents or heirs cannot claim prescriptive title against the owner or his heirs on the basis of the period he possessed the property.",SRC029-s116,section-not-found,Section 116 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_242_2016_amd1-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_242_2016_amd1-1.pdf,,SC,2016,A person who enters into occupation of property as a licensee cannot acquire prescriptive right to the property unless they produce an event or overt act that manifests their intention to occupy the property as the owner.,"A person, who is let into occupation of property as a tenant, or as a licensee, must be deemed to continue to occupy on the footing on which he was admitted, until by some overt act by which he manifests his intention of occupying it in another capacity.",SRC071-s13,ok-unchecked,Section 13 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,The possession of a property by a defendant is considered 'undisturbed and uninterrupted possession' if they have been in continuous possession for more than ten years.,"In the presence of the above evidence, it is not difficult to hold that the possession of this property by Defendant is ‘undisturbed and uninterrupted possession’ within the meaning of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_30_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_30_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,Time cannot run against remaindermen in terms of section 3 of the Ordinance.,"As explained above, time cannot run against remaindermen in terms of section 3 of the Ordinance.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_32_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_32_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,A party cannot take up new positions which are questions of fact for the first time on appeal.,The defendants cannot take up new positions which are questions of fact for the first time on appeal.,SRC029-s114,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A person cannot acquire prescriptive title without demonstrating an overt act showing an intention to possess adversely to the owner.,"Without any demonstration of any overt act against the rights of the owner, court cannot recognize Nandasekera as a person who has acted as one holding prescriptive possession against the owner who had given him leave and license to step in and live there, no matter how long he had enjoyed the property.",SRC029-s3,section-not-found,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A deed required by law to be attested must be proved in terms of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance if it is impeached or challenged through an issue raised.,"However, if the deed is impeached or challenged through an issue raised, it has to be proved as per the provisions of Evidence Ordinance.",SRC029-s68,section-not-found,section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_39_2024.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_39_2024.pdf,,SC,2024,A successful action for declaration of title interrupts prescription.,"A successful action for declaration of title interrupts prescription and therefore, when the appellant was successful in case 17819/L, by Judgment dated 12.06.2009, the plea of prescriptive title by the respondent fail, as during the pendency of the successful action, the respondents have failed to be in uninterrupted and undisturbed possession for 10 years.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A licensee cannot challenge the title of the owner of the property in suit.,"A licencee as the Defendant who entered the property in dispute with the leave and licence of Plaintiff cannot in law challenge the title of the owner of the property in suit, in this case the Plaintiff party.",SRC029-s116,section-not-found,Section 116 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_48_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_48_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A servitude may be acquired either by express grant or by prescription.,A servitude may be acquired either by express grant or by prescription.,SRC001-s2,ok,Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_49_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_49_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A notary must personally know the executant and be in a position to bear witness to the fact that the signature on the deed executed before him is the signature of the executant.,To become an attesting witness a notary must personally know the executant and be in a position to bear witness to the fact that the signature on the deed executed before him is the signature of the executant.,SRC001-s2,ok,section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2007.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2007.pdf,,SC,2007,An acknowledgment of a debt made after the limitation period has run is valid and effective to start time running again.,"In view of my finding that the Prescription Ordinance merely bars the remedy with lapse of time and does not extinguish the debt, there is no rational justification to insist that an acknowledgment must be made before the expiry of the limitation period to be effective. I hold that an acknowledgment of the debt to be effective for the purposes of section 12 of the Prescription Ordinance need not be made before the expiry of the period of limitation.",SRC071-s12,ok-unchecked,section 12 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The District Court cannot permit the Plaintiff to add a party after the prescriptive period.,The District Court could not permit the Plaintiff to add a party after the prescriptive period.,SRC071-s9,ok-unchecked,section 9 of the Prescription Ordinance No.22 of 1871,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A contract may be avoided by Court on the ground of laesio enormis either when the purchaser pays more than double the true value of the thing or the vendor sells the thing for less than half its value.,This inequality between the value of the thing and the price paid is termed laesio enormis. A contract may be avoided by Court on the ground of laesio enormis either when the purchaser pays more than double the true value of the thing or the vendor sells the thing for less than half its value.,SRC001-s2,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,"If the original amount of the loan granted was less than Rs.5 million, the bank cannot, in a case of default, sell the property mortgaged by public auction.","Prior to and after the enactment of Act No.19 of 2011, if the original amount of the loan granted was less than Rs.5 million, the bank cannot, in a case of default, sell the property mortgaged by public auction;",SRC001-s5A,section-not-found,Section 5A of the Recovery of Loans by Banks (Special Provisions) Act No.4 of 1990 as amended by Act No.1of 2011 and Act No.19 of 2011,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A party invoking the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights with consistent and credible evidence.,"In order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights (Chelliah vs. Wijenathan 54 NLR 337, Solomon Dias vs. William Singho and others [2015] 1 SLR 277).",SRC071-s3,none,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_54_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_54_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"A bank is entitled to pass a resolution to sell a mortgaged property to recover the total amount due on multiple loans, even if the borrower has not defaulted on one of the loans, as long as the loans were granted on the security of the same property.","If the said section is interpreted to mean that, when two or more loans have been granted on the security of the same property, a bank is not entitled to sell the mortgaged property to recover the money due on loans that are being defaulted merely because the other loans have been serviced without any default, the bank would not be able to recover the money due on the defaulting loan. Such an interpretation would render the said section nugatory.",SRC004-s12,section-not-found,section 12 of the Recovery of Loans by Banks (Special Provisions) Act No. 4 of 1990,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_54a_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_54a_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A person cannot acquire prescriptive title to a property if they have not demonstrated an intention to possess the property adversely to and independent of the owner's title, and have not manifested that intention to the owner.","To again apply the words of Bertram CJ in in TILLEKERATNE vs. BASTIAN, the defendant has not demonstrated to the plaintiff‟s father that the defendant had “an intention on his [her] part to possess adversely” and had not demonstrated “a manifestation of that intention” to the plaintiff‟s father.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A court must consider all prayers in a plaint when deciding on an ex parte judgment.,"The learned District Judge has not stated, in his judgment, that the Plaintiff also seeks a declaration to the effect that deed No 1014 dated 29.12.1994 attested by IM Wimalasena is a valid deed.",SRC029-s68,section-not-found,section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_56_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_56_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,"A notary can be an attesting witness even if he does not know the executant personally, but proof of execution of the deed is incomplete on his evidence alone.",It is in that context the Court held that the notary was not an attesting witness. This should not be understood to mean that a notary can never be an attesting witness unless he knows the executant personally.,SRC001-s2,ok,section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_58_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_58_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A person who possesses a land with secret intention cannot claim benefit of the law of the land.,A person who possesses a land with secret intention cannot claim that his possession is an adverse possession.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_59_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_59_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,"A court must issue notice on respondents when a prima facie case of an illegality is established, warranting full investigation with the participation of all parties.","In order to have notice issued on the Respondents, the burden cast on the Appellant was to establish a prima facie sustainable case and for the Court to be satisfied that there is a prima facie case to be looked into.",SRC029-s5,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_5_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_5_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A party claiming prescription must prove that there was undisturbed adverse possession for 10 years.,"Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance provides, “ Proof of the undisturbed and uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of lands or immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff in such action (that is to say, a possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce, or performance of service or duty, or by any other act by the possessor, from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred) for ten years previous to the bringing of such action, shall entitle the defendant to a decree in his favour with costs...”",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_5_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_5_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A licensee of an owner of a property cannot acquire prescriptive title to the property against the owner of the property so long as he holds the status of a licensee.,I hold that licensee of an owner of a property cannot acquire prescriptive title to the property against the owner of the property so long as he holds the status of a licensee.,SRC029-s116,section-not-found,Section 116 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_60_2004.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_60_2004.pdf,,SC,2004,The failure to challenge evidence by cross-examination may be a factor to be taken into account in accepting such evidence.,"However, in MMBL Teas (Pvt) Ltd. v. British Ceylon Produce Export (Pvt) Ltd. [S.C. Case No. SC/CHC/35/2008, S.C.M. 17.12.2021] Aluwihare, PC, J., (at page 17) was of the view that failure to challenge evidence by cross examination by itself may not be sufficient to hold that a particular fact had been proved within the meaning of Section 3 of the Evidence Ordinance. It might, however, be a factor to be taken into account in accepting such evidence.",SRC029-s2,ok,Section 3 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_62_2024.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_62_2024.pdf,,SC,2024,"The provisions of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance (particularly Section 2 and related sections) are intended to prevent frauds and perjuries, as stated in its preamble, and should not be invoked to conceal the truth or defeat justice.","The provisions of the said Prevention of Frauds Ordinance (particularly Section 2 and related sections) are intended to prevent frauds and perjuries, as stated in its preamble, and should not be invoked to conceal the truth or defeat justice.",SRC001-s2,ok,section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_66_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_66_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,The fact that the Petitioner-Appellant has not signed the letter dated 21-09-2002 X 10 is not relevant as he does not have soil rights to the by-lane.,His right of way can continue without any hindrance even after vesting the by-lane in the Urban Council as a public road.,SRC042-s52,section-not-found,Section 52 of the Urban Councils Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_68_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_68_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A settlement between a plaintiff and a defendant in a previous rei vindicatio action cannot bind a third party who acquired rights from the vendor.,The settlement cannot bind the vendee Nalika Jeewanthi as she was not a party to the settlement.,SRC004-s35,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_68_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_68_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,The burden of proving that a property is Thediathetam is on the party who claims it to be so.,I am of the view that the burden of proving that a property is Thediathetam is on the party who claims it to be so;,SRC029-s19,section-not-found,Section 106 of the Evidence Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_69_2019-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_69_2019-1.pdf,,SC,2019,A party claiming prescriptive title must demonstrate a clear separation of the land in question from the remainder of the property.,"There was no cogent evidence led to demonstrate a clear separation of Lot B, on the northern boundary, from the remainder of the land.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A well-defined and identifiable course or track should have been adversely used by the owner of the dominant tenement for over ten years to acquire a right of way by prescription.,It is a prerequisite to the acquisition of a right of way by prescription that a well-defined and identifiable course or track should have been adversely used by the owner of the dominant tenement for over ten years,SRC071-s3,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A co-owner may sue another co-owner of an adjoining land for the definition of boundaries without the participation of other co-owners.,"It is a well-established principle of law that a co-owner could sue a trespasser without the participation of other co-owners. (Hevawitrane v. Dangan Rubber Co., Ltd. 17 NLR 49; Arnolisa et al v. Dissan et al 4 NLR 163; Meera Lebbe Cassi Lebbe Marikkar v. Kalavilage Baba (1885) 7 S.C.C.) If a co-owner could sue a trespasser, in the same way a co-owner should be able to be sued without participation of other co-owners.",SRC029-s115,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_74_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_74_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A subsequent purchase of title by a party claiming prescription to the same land does not negate the prescriptive title acquired by such party to such land.,"any subsequent purchase of title by a party claiming prescription to the same land, does not negate the prescriptive title acquired by such party to such land.",SRC071-s3,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_75_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_75_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A party cannot acquire prescriptive title to a portion of a road if the final decree in a partition action has already wiped out all such rights.,"Nor can they claim any prescriptive rights to that portion because the Final Decree P4 wiped out all such rights, if they had any.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_7_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_7_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,"A party can acquire prescriptive title to land by possessing it as a separate land for several decades, independent of the title of the original owners.",There is no doubt that the 4th Defendant has prescribed to Lots D and E by a title adverse to or independent of that of the Plaintiff and the 1st to 3rd Defendants as required by section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_80_2022.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_80_2022.pdf,,SC,2022,"For the defendant to prove 'adverse' possession, there should have been an intention by him to claim title to the land against its owner.","There must be a corporeal occupation of land attended with a manifest intention to hold and continue it and when the intent plainly is to hold the land against the claim of all other persons, the possession is hostile or adverse to the rights of the true owner",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance No. 22 of 1871 (as amended),True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_83_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_83_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A possessory action can be filed if the plaintiff is disturbed but not ousted from the land in their occupation.,Disturbance of possession was protected by the mandament van maintenue. The applicant for such a relief had to give a concise account of his possession and of the disturbance caused by the other party.,SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,Section 4 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_94_2025.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_94_2025.pdf,,SC,2025,"To establish prescriptive acquisition of immovable property, a defendant must prove undisturbed and uninterrupted possession for more than 10 years.","Proof of the undisturbed and uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of lands or immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff in such action (that is to say, a possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce, or performance of service or duty, or by any other act by the possessor, from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred) for ten years previous to the bringing of such action, shall entitle the defendant to a decree in his favour with costs.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_96_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_96_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,The progress of prescription is not arrested by the minority of the plaintiffs or the absence of their father beyond the seas.,"the mere fact that his succession passed to the plaintiffs on his death, and that they were minors at the time, cannot arrest the progress of prescription.",SRC071-s13,ok-unchecked,Section 13 of the Prescription Ordinance No. 2 of 1889,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_1_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_1_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,Claims arising out of goods sold and delivered become prescribed in 1 year.,"As observed by the learned Judge of the High Court, in terms of section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance, claims arising out of such transactions become prescribed in 1 year.",SRC071-s8,ok-unchecked,section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_22_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_22_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A contract for the sale of goods requires a sale and delivery of the goods within a year for the prescriptive period to apply.,"No action shall be maintainable for or in respect of any goods sold and delivered, or for any shop bill or book debt, or for work and labour done, or for the wages of artisans, laborers, or servants, unless the same shall be brought within one year after the debt shall have become due.",SRC071-s8,ok-unchecked,Section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_23_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_23_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,The prescription period for an action without a written agreement is three years from the date of the cause of action.,"In the case of an unwritten agreement, an action should be filed within three years from the breach of the said agreement (from the date of the cause of action), in terms of section 7 of the said Ordinance.",SRC071-s7,ok-unchecked,section 7 of the Prescription Ordinance No. 22 of 1871 as amended,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_24_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_24_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,A surety who has undertaken liability as co-principal debtor has impliedly and tacitly renounced their right to plead the defence of beneficium ordinis seu excussionis.,"I am therefore of the view that by undertaking liability as co-principal debtor, the Defendants have impliedly as well as tacitly renounced their right to plead the defence of beneficium ordinis seu excussionis.",SRC029-s101,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_3_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_3_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,An action based on a written contract is not prescribed if brought within six years from the date of the breach of such contract.,"Section 6 of the Prescription Ordinance provides that; “No action shall be maintainable upon ... any written promise, contract, bargain, or agreement, ... unless such action shall be brought within six years from the date of the breach of such ... contract, bargain or agreement, ... from the date when such note or bill shall have become due, or of the last payment of interest thereon.”",SRC071-s6,ok-unchecked,Section 6 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_7_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_7_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"An action to recover a debt based on an account stated is not prescribed in three years, but in one year if it is based on goods sold and delivered.",An action to recover the balance was prescribed in three years.,SRC071-s7,ok-unchecked,Section 7 of the Prescription Ordinance,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_fr_13_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_fr_13_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,A person who has agreed to purchase a house on an installment basis can use the agreement as proof of residence.,"When considering the totality of the evidence placed before this Court, I hold that the 1st Petitioner is entitled to obtain full marks to the document produce marked as 9A, the main document in proof of the Residence of the Petitioner.",SRC001-s4,none,,False,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_fr_311_2016-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_fr_311_2016-1.pdf,,SC,2016,A public officer's transfer must be based on a valid reason and the officer must be made aware of the reason.,"The PSC Rules recognise three different categories of exigencies of service, so merely stating that it was on exigencies of service is not sufficient.",SRC029-s114(f),none,Article 12 (1) of the Constitution,True,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1977-4-1,commonlii-LKCA-1977-4,80 NLR 256,LKCA,1977,A misdescription of land boundaries in a deed does not invalidate the deed.,"That as the evidence and the findings of the Trial Judge show that the assessment numbers given in the respective deeds are the correct assessment numbers of the allotment of land each party intended to buy and in fact purchased and was in possession of, the description of the land by the boundaries was a misdescription which although it would not invalidate the deeds, could be rejected or ignored. The maxim falsa demonstrate non nocet which is embodied in section 95 of the Evidence Ordinance applied. Per Pathirana, J. ""The Assessment numbers provide a definite and sufficient description of the land in the respective deeds of the plaintiff and the defendant of what was intendend to pass and the descriptions in those deeds by boundaries are erroneous"". A PPEAL from the judgment of the District Court of Kandy. H. W. Jayewardene with B, J. Fernando and Miss S. Senaratne for Defendant-Appellant. T. B. Dissanayake for Plaintiff-Respondent",SRC029-s95,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1984-24-1,commonlii-LKCA-1984-24,(1985) 2 Sri LR 35,LKCA,1984,A party to a suit cannot set up the title of a third party who is not their predecessor in title and who has not been joined in the action.,A party to a suit cannot under s. 3 of the Prescription Ordinance set up the title of a third party who is not his predecessor in title and who has not been joined in the action. The judgment in a case must be declaratory of the right of a party to the suit not of a stranger,SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1986-39-1,commonlii-LKCA-1986-39,(1986) 1 Sri LR 378,LKCA,1986,"In a vindicatory suit, the plaintiff must prove their own title.","In a vindicatory suit the plaintiff must prove his title. Having failed to prove his own title, he cannot rely on the weakness of the 1st defendant's title. Whatever the strength of the 1st defendant's case, if the plaintiff fails to establish his title, plaintiff's case must necessarily fail",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1988-51-1,commonlii-LKCA-1988-51,(1988) 2 Sri LR 264,LKCA,1988,A declaration of title cannot be sought on different media than those on which the title was originally based.,"(1) The Media on which the plaintiff now seeks a declaration of title are different from. the media on which he, based his title in the earlier case: The cause of action is different. (2) Prescriptive title has to be re-considered on the basis of the new character in which the plaintiff figures in the second action. Plaintiff would have passing to him whatever title by prescription which Jefferjee had and this differs from the prescriptive title he claimed in the earlier case",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1988-51-2,commonlii-LKCA-1988-51,(1988) 2 Sri LR 264,LKCA,1988,A prescriptive title must be re-considered based on the new character in which the plaintiff figures in a subsequent action.,"(1) The Media on which the plaintiff now seeks a declaration of title are different from. the media on which he, based his title in the earlier case: The cause of action is different. (2) Prescriptive title has to be re-considered on the basis of the new character in which the plaintiff figures in the second action. Plaintiff would have passing to him whatever title by prescription which Jefferjee had and this differs from the prescriptive title he claimed in the earlier case",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1998-51-1,commonlii-LKCA-1998-51,(1998) 3 Sri LR 46,LKCA,1998,A contract for a building lease ends if the building is burnt down without the fault of the landlord or tenant.,"1. Where a building which is the subject matter is burnt down without the fault of the landlord or tenant, the contract is at an end. 2. The trial Judge on a preponderance of evidence led came to a finding that the plaintiff-appellant had set fire to the house. This is a finding of primary fact by a trial Judge who had seen and heard the witness based upon the credibility of such witness, which is entitled to great weight and utmost consideration. 3. The order of the Rent Board was based on the premise that there existed a building which could be repaired. In the case of a house being let if that is completely burnt, the lease comes to an end, but not where the tenant is able to exercise, many of his rights under the lease notwithstanding the complete destruction of the buildings",SRC029-s145(1),none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1999-83-1,commonlii-LKCA-1999-83,(2000) 3 Sri LR 23,LKCA,1999,"In a usufructuary mortgage, prescription can only run from a breach of the condition related to the use and enjoyment of the property.",(1) A usufructuary mortgage being a devise for use and enjoyment of property in lieu of interest - prescription could run only from a breach of that condition. (2) Person who entered property in a subordinate character cannot claim prescriptive rights till he changes his character by an overt act. The proof of adverse possession is a condition precedent to the claim for prescriptive rights. (3) Usufructuary mortgagee is bound to the terms of the Bond itself and his rights are confined to the terms in the bond. He cannot claim prescriptive rights till he changes his character and claim adversely disregarding the conditions of the Bond. There is no question of merger of the entirety of the rights and no prescriptive rights being acquired by the Mortgagee by adverse possession,SRC071-s5,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1999-83-2,commonlii-LKCA-1999-83,(2000) 3 Sri LR 23,LKCA,1999,A person who enters a property in a subordinate character cannot claim prescriptive rights until they change their character by an overt act.,(1) A usufructuary mortgage being a devise for use and enjoyment of property in lieu of interest - prescription could run only from a breach of that condition. (2) Person who entered property in a subordinate character cannot claim prescriptive rights till he changes his character by an overt act. The proof of adverse possession is a condition precedent to the claim for prescriptive rights. (3) Usufructuary mortgagee is bound to the terms of the Bond itself and his rights are confined to the terms in the bond. He cannot claim prescriptive rights till he changes his character and claim adversely disregarding the conditions of the Bond. There is no question of merger of the entirety of the rights and no prescriptive rights being acquired by the Mortgagee by adverse possession,SRC071-s5,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1999-83-3,commonlii-LKCA-1999-83,(2000) 3 Sri LR 23,LKCA,1999,A usufructuary mortgagee is bound by the terms of the bond and their rights are limited to those terms.,(1) A usufructuary mortgage being a devise for use and enjoyment of property in lieu of interest - prescription could run only from a breach of that condition. (2) Person who entered property in a subordinate character cannot claim prescriptive rights till he changes his character by an overt act. The proof of adverse possession is a condition precedent to the claim for prescriptive rights. (3) Usufructuary mortgagee is bound to the terms of the Bond itself and his rights are confined to the terms in the bond. He cannot claim prescriptive rights till he changes his character and claim adversely disregarding the conditions of the Bond. There is no question of merger of the entirety of the rights and no prescriptive rights being acquired by the Mortgagee by adverse possession,SRC071-s5,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2001-1-1,commonlii-LKCA-2001-1,(2002) 2 Sri LR 384,LKCA,2001,A party's prescription of land is determined by the land's physical boundaries as seen from the land itself.,"The appellant has prescribed to the land as seen from - (1) Continued possession of the corpus after the death of E and from 1969 after the widow left the corpus. (2) Planting with coconuts, etc., and enjoying the produce. (3) Friends and relations arranging a marriage in 1970, with the marriage ceremony being taken in the house and premises in suit. (4) The widow of E not attending the marriage ceremony. (5) Building extensions, effecting repairs, renting a portion of the house in 1970. 385 (6) Continued possession after marriage. (7) Payment of rates and taxes from 1973. (8) Long and undisturbed possession of the corpus been corroborated by the evidence of the Grama Sevaka",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2001-1-2,commonlii-LKCA-2001-1,(2002) 2 Sri LR 384,LKCA,2001,Continued possession of the corpus after the death of E and from 1969 after the widow left the corpus is relevant.,"The appellant has prescribed to the land as seen from - (1) Continued possession of the corpus after the death of E and from 1969 after the widow left the corpus. (2) Planting with coconuts, etc., and enjoying the produce. (3) Friends and relations arranging a marriage in 1970, with the marriage ceremony being taken in the house and premises in suit. (4) The widow of E not attending the marriage ceremony. (5) Building extensions, effecting repairs, renting a portion of the house in 1970. 385 (6) Continued possession after marriage. (7) Payment of rates and taxes from 1973. (8) Long and undisturbed possession of the corpus been corroborated by the evidence of the Grama Sevaka",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,fact-specific,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2001-1-3,commonlii-LKCA-2001-1,(2002) 2 Sri LR 384,LKCA,2001,"Planting with coconuts, etc., and enjoying the produce is a relevant activity.","The appellant has prescribed to the land as seen from - (1) Continued possession of the corpus after the death of E and from 1969 after the widow left the corpus. (2) Planting with coconuts, etc., and enjoying the produce. (3) Friends and relations arranging a marriage in 1970, with the marriage ceremony being taken in the house and premises in suit. (4) The widow of E not attending the marriage ceremony. (5) Building extensions, effecting repairs, renting a portion of the house in 1970. 385 (6) Continued possession after marriage. (7) Payment of rates and taxes from 1973. (8) Long and undisturbed possession of the corpus been corroborated by the evidence of the Grama Sevaka",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,fact-specific,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2001-99-1,commonlii-LKCA-2001-99,(2002) 1 Sri LR 148,LKCA,2001,The right of dominium of the plaintiffs is in contest with the declaration of adverse possession by the defendants.,"(1) The contest is between the right of dominium of the plaintiffs and the declaration of adverse possession amounting to prescription by the defendants. (2) The moment title is proved the right to possess it, is presumed. (3) Thus, even if the Court found that the defendants had prescribed to the corpus, the proper answer to the 1st issue would have been yes, but the defendants have prescribed. (4) An averment of prescription by a plaintiff in a plaint after pleading paper title is employed only to buttress his paper title. (5) For the Court to have come to its decision as to whether the plaintiff had dominium, the proving of paper title is sufficient. 149 (6) The mere fact that the plaintiff claimed both on deeds as well as by long possession did not entail the plaintiff to prove prescriptive title thereto. Their possession was presumed on proving paper title. The averment of prescription in the plaint did not cast any burden upon the plaintiff to prove a separate title by prescription in addition to paper title. (7) Once paper title became undisputed the burden shifted to the defendants to show that they had independent rights in the form of prescription as claimed by them. Per Wigneswaran, J. ""A person is in possession of a house for example, when he or his servants or licensees are living in it, if he or they are absent from it, he would still be held to be in possession, if such absence was only temporary. In this case the brother of the 1st plaintiff (1st defendant) could have been in occupation and still the 1st plaintiff would have been in possession simultaneously.""",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2001-99-2,commonlii-LKCA-2001-99,(2002) 1 Sri LR 148,LKCA,2001,The right to possess a title is presumed once it is proved.,"(1) The contest is between the right of dominium of the plaintiffs and the declaration of adverse possession amounting to prescription by the defendants. (2) The moment title is proved the right to possess it, is presumed. (3) Thus, even if the Court found that the defendants had prescribed to the corpus, the proper answer to the 1st issue would have been yes, but the defendants have prescribed. (4) An averment of prescription by a plaintiff in a plaint after pleading paper title is employed only to buttress his paper title. (5) For the Court to have come to its decision as to whether the plaintiff had dominium, the proving of paper title is sufficient. 149 (6) The mere fact that the plaintiff claimed both on deeds as well as by long possession did not entail the plaintiff to prove prescriptive title thereto. Their possession was presumed on proving paper title. The averment of prescription in the plaint did not cast any burden upon the plaintiff to prove a separate title by prescription in addition to paper title. (7) Once paper title became undisputed the burden shifted to the defendants to show that they had independent rights in the form of prescription as claimed by them. Per Wigneswaran, J. ""A person is in possession of a house for example, when he or his servants or licensees are living in it, if he or they are absent from it, he would still be held to be in possession, if such absence was only temporary. In this case the brother of the 1st plaintiff (1st defendant) could have been in occupation and still the 1st plaintiff would have been in possession simultaneously.""",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2001-99-3,commonlii-LKCA-2001-99,(2002) 1 Sri LR 148,LKCA,2001,"Even if the defendants had prescribed to the corpus, the proper answer to the 1st issue would have been yes.","(1) The contest is between the right of dominium of the plaintiffs and the declaration of adverse possession amounting to prescription by the defendants. (2) The moment title is proved the right to possess it, is presumed. (3) Thus, even if the Court found that the defendants had prescribed to the corpus, the proper answer to the 1st issue would have been yes, but the defendants have prescribed. (4) An averment of prescription by a plaintiff in a plaint after pleading paper title is employed only to buttress his paper title. (5) For the Court to have come to its decision as to whether the plaintiff had dominium, the proving of paper title is sufficient. 149 (6) The mere fact that the plaintiff claimed both on deeds as well as by long possession did not entail the plaintiff to prove prescriptive title thereto. Their possession was presumed on proving paper title. The averment of prescription in the plaint did not cast any burden upon the plaintiff to prove a separate title by prescription in addition to paper title. (7) Once paper title became undisputed the burden shifted to the defendants to show that they had independent rights in the form of prescription as claimed by them. Per Wigneswaran, J. ""A person is in possession of a house for example, when he or his servants or licensees are living in it, if he or they are absent from it, he would still be held to be in possession, if such absence was only temporary. In this case the brother of the 1st plaintiff (1st defendant) could have been in occupation and still the 1st plaintiff would have been in possession simultaneously.""",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2002-76-1,commonlii-LKCA-2002-76,(2002) 3 Sri LR 201,LKCA,2002,A tenant under the Rent Act is entitled to seek statutory protection.,(1) The defendant-appellant being a tenant under the plaintiff is entitled to seek protection under the provisions of the Rent Act. Such statutory protection comes to an end only upon (a) by handing back of the premises to the landlord or (b) by order of a competent court. It is open to a tenant to recall a promise to surrender possession. (2) The common law rights of a landlord to institute action for an order of ejectment of a tenant is curtailed by the provisions of the Rent Act. (3) A conditional agreement cannot form the basis for an order of ejectment. A PPEAL from the judgment of the District Court of Mt. Lavinia. 202,SRC001-s2,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2002-76-2,commonlii-LKCA-2002-76,(2002) 3 Sri LR 201,LKCA,2002,The common law rights of a landlord to institute action for an order of ejectment of a tenant are curtailed.,(1) The defendant-appellant being a tenant under the plaintiff is entitled to seek protection under the provisions of the Rent Act. Such statutory protection comes to an end only upon (a) by handing back of the premises to the landlord or (b) by order of a competent court. It is open to a tenant to recall a promise to surrender possession. (2) The common law rights of a landlord to institute action for an order of ejectment of a tenant is curtailed by the provisions of the Rent Act. (3) A conditional agreement cannot form the basis for an order of ejectment. A PPEAL from the judgment of the District Court of Mt. Lavinia. 202,SRC001-s2,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2002-76-3,commonlii-LKCA-2002-76,(2002) 3 Sri LR 201,LKCA,2002,A conditional agreement cannot be used as the basis for an order of ejectment.,(1) The defendant-appellant being a tenant under the plaintiff is entitled to seek protection under the provisions of the Rent Act. Such statutory protection comes to an end only upon (a) by handing back of the premises to the landlord or (b) by order of a competent court. It is open to a tenant to recall a promise to surrender possession. (2) The common law rights of a landlord to institute action for an order of ejectment of a tenant is curtailed by the provisions of the Rent Act. (3) A conditional agreement cannot form the basis for an order of ejectment. A PPEAL from the judgment of the District Court of Mt. Lavinia. 202,SRC001-s2,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2003-66-1,commonlii-LKCA-2003-66,(2003) 2 Sri LR 341,LKCA,2003,Privity of contract is the foundation of the right to relief in an action by a lessor against an overholding lessee.,"(i) Privity of contract is the foundation of the right to relief in an action by a lessor against an over holding lessee for restoration and ejectment and issues as to title are irrelevant. A lessee who has entered into occupation is precluded from disputing his lessor's title until he has first restored the property in fulfillment of his contractual obligations. (ii) The plaintiff who claims qua landlord to eject his tenant in occupation, must establish that privity of contract between himself and the tenant exists at the relevant date, and if it does exist the tenant is precluded by provisions of section 116 of the Evidence Ordinance from disputing the plaintiff's title to the premises",SRC029-s116,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2003-66-2,commonlii-LKCA-2003-66,(2003) 2 Sri LR 341,LKCA,2003,A plaintiff claiming to be a landlord must establish the existence of privity of contract with the tenant at the relevant date.,"(i) Privity of contract is the foundation of the right to relief in an action by a lessor against an over holding lessee for restoration and ejectment and issues as to title are irrelevant. A lessee who has entered into occupation is precluded from disputing his lessor's title until he has first restored the property in fulfillment of his contractual obligations. (ii) The plaintiff who claims qua landlord to eject his tenant in occupation, must establish that privity of contract between himself and the tenant exists at the relevant date, and if it does exist the tenant is precluded by provisions of section 116 of the Evidence Ordinance from disputing the plaintiff's title to the premises",SRC029-s116,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2007-3-1,commonlii-LKCA-2007-3,(2007) 1 Sri LR 13,LKCA,2007,"A party asserting fraud does not have to prove the fact, but rather the burden is on the other party to dispel all suspicion.",(1) Every circumstance that is to be considered by Court need not be raised by way of an issue. The real issue that the petitioner was required to meet was whether the impugned last will was a forgery. The allegation of fraud with regard to a last will is an instance which finds an exception to the rule of evidence that the party asserting (fraud) has to prove the fact. The Rule set up by Courts through the line of authorities is that when there is a suggestion of fraud even if the evidence is not of such a nature as to justify a finding of fraud the burden is cast on the propounder of the Will to dispel all suspicion if he were to have the will proved and probate granted. (2) In considering the judgment as a whole and not part by part it is clear that the trial judge did not reject and dismiss the,SRC029-s101,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-2007-3-2,commonlii-LKCA-2007-3,(2007) 1 Sri LR 13,LKCA,2007,A judgment should be considered as a whole rather than part by part.,(1) Every circumstance that is to be considered by Court need not be raised by way of an issue. The real issue that the petitioner was required to meet was whether the impugned last will was a forgery. The allegation of fraud with regard to a last will is an instance which finds an exception to the rule of evidence that the party asserting (fraud) has to prove the fact. The Rule set up by Courts through the line of authorities is that when there is a suggestion of fraud even if the evidence is not of such a nature as to justify a finding of fraud the burden is cast on the propounder of the Will to dispel all suspicion if he were to have the will proved and probate granted. (2) In considering the judgment as a whole and not part by part it is clear that the trial judge did not reject and dismiss the,SRC029-s101,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1979-5-1,commonlii-LKSC-1979-5,(1995) 1 Sri LR 7,LKSC,1979,A person who holds themselves out as an agent of another may be presumed to be a sufficient medium for receiving notice on behalf of the principal.,"(1) The 1st defendant had held himself out as the agent of the other defendants and the other defendants by their own conduct held out that the 1st defendant was their sole spokesman in relation to the transaction so much as to induce Gnanam, Managing Director of the plaintiff company and the lawyers for the company that the 1st defendant was a sufficient medium through which notice could reach the other defendants. The 1st defendant did not give evidence and the court is entitled to draw the presumption that had he given evidence; such evidence would have been unfavourable to the case of the defendants - see section 114 illustration (f) of the Evidence Ordinance. (2) The draft deed of transfer was received by 1st defendant four days prior to the date fixed for signing the transfer. There was no uncertainty in relation to the vendors and vendee, the subject matter of the sale and the consideration. There was sufficient compliance on the part of the plaintiff in regard to the stipulation, of notice of the, draft. Although a plaintiff who breaks-an-essential term of the agreement cannot claim specific performance, trivial breaches do not disentitle a party from claiming relief. (3) Time was of the essence of the contract only in respect of the last date for signing the deed of transfer and in no other respect On the last date for signing, the defendants were not available and wanted a further date to sign the deed of transfer. 8 (4) The plaintiff was entitled to a decree for specific performance",SRC029-s114,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1979-5-2,commonlii-LKSC-1979-5,(1995) 1 Sri LR 7,LKSC,1979,A party who breaks an essential term of an agreement cannot claim specific performance.,"(1) The 1st defendant had held himself out as the agent of the other defendants and the other defendants by their own conduct held out that the 1st defendant was their sole spokesman in relation to the transaction so much as to induce Gnanam, Managing Director of the plaintiff company and the lawyers for the company that the 1st defendant was a sufficient medium through which notice could reach the other defendants. The 1st defendant did not give evidence and the court is entitled to draw the presumption that had he given evidence; such evidence would have been unfavourable to the case of the defendants - see section 114 illustration (f) of the Evidence Ordinance. (2) The draft deed of transfer was received by 1st defendant four days prior to the date fixed for signing the transfer. There was no uncertainty in relation to the vendors and vendee, the subject matter of the sale and the consideration. There was sufficient compliance on the part of the plaintiff in regard to the stipulation, of notice of the, draft. Although a plaintiff who breaks-an-essential term of the agreement cannot claim specific performance, trivial breaches do not disentitle a party from claiming relief. (3) Time was of the essence of the contract only in respect of the last date for signing the deed of transfer and in no other respect On the last date for signing, the defendants were not available and wanted a further date to sign the deed of transfer. 8 (4) The plaintiff was entitled to a decree for specific performance",SRC029-s114,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1979-5-3,commonlii-LKSC-1979-5,(1995) 1 Sri LR 7,LKSC,1979,Time is of the essence of a contract only in respect of specific dates agreed upon by the parties.,"(1) The 1st defendant had held himself out as the agent of the other defendants and the other defendants by their own conduct held out that the 1st defendant was their sole spokesman in relation to the transaction so much as to induce Gnanam, Managing Director of the plaintiff company and the lawyers for the company that the 1st defendant was a sufficient medium through which notice could reach the other defendants. The 1st defendant did not give evidence and the court is entitled to draw the presumption that had he given evidence; such evidence would have been unfavourable to the case of the defendants - see section 114 illustration (f) of the Evidence Ordinance. (2) The draft deed of transfer was received by 1st defendant four days prior to the date fixed for signing the transfer. There was no uncertainty in relation to the vendors and vendee, the subject matter of the sale and the consideration. There was sufficient compliance on the part of the plaintiff in regard to the stipulation, of notice of the, draft. Although a plaintiff who breaks-an-essential term of the agreement cannot claim specific performance, trivial breaches do not disentitle a party from claiming relief. (3) Time was of the essence of the contract only in respect of the last date for signing the deed of transfer and in no other respect On the last date for signing, the defendants were not available and wanted a further date to sign the deed of transfer. 8 (4) The plaintiff was entitled to a decree for specific performance",SRC029-s114,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1993-49-1,commonlii-LKSC-1993-49,(1994) 3 Sri LR 245,LKSC,1993,The amalgamation of a lot with another land is possible.,"(1) Amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda was possible and the corpus (Batadombagahawatta) did not include lots 5B, 5C, 5D and 6B and should be excluded. (2) The causes of the 3rd defendant and 4th defendant had to be considered separately. The deeds of the 3rd defendant referred to undivided interests in the whole land, while the deed by which' Ratnayake's widow conveyed her interests to the 4th defendant referred to interests in a divided lot. (3) The amicable partition on Plan 1053 with the amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda in 1951, significant improvement of lot 5 by planting the entirety of lot 5 (except the paddy portion and a narrow slip adjoining the main road) and the 4th defendant's dealing with lot 5 as a distinct entity along with the continuous exclusive, undisturbed, adverse and uninterrupted possession by herself and her predecessors in title prove her claim to have prescribed to lot 5. (4) The partition should be confined to lots 3, 4 and 7 as shown in the preliminary plan but 4th defendant will not be entitled to any shares. (5) The interests of Tudor, one of the three children of the 3rd defendant, will remain unallotted if the 3rd defendant, on being given an opportunity, fails to prove that these rights passed to her",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1993-49-2,commonlii-LKSC-1993-49,(1994) 3 Sri LR 245,LKSC,1993,The causes of separate defendants must be considered separately.,"(1) Amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda was possible and the corpus (Batadombagahawatta) did not include lots 5B, 5C, 5D and 6B and should be excluded. (2) The causes of the 3rd defendant and 4th defendant had to be considered separately. The deeds of the 3rd defendant referred to undivided interests in the whole land, while the deed by which' Ratnayake's widow conveyed her interests to the 4th defendant referred to interests in a divided lot. (3) The amicable partition on Plan 1053 with the amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda in 1951, significant improvement of lot 5 by planting the entirety of lot 5 (except the paddy portion and a narrow slip adjoining the main road) and the 4th defendant's dealing with lot 5 as a distinct entity along with the continuous exclusive, undisturbed, adverse and uninterrupted possession by herself and her predecessors in title prove her claim to have prescribed to lot 5. (4) The partition should be confined to lots 3, 4 and 7 as shown in the preliminary plan but 4th defendant will not be entitled to any shares. (5) The interests of Tudor, one of the three children of the 3rd defendant, will remain unallotted if the 3rd defendant, on being given an opportunity, fails to prove that these rights passed to her",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1993-49-3,commonlii-LKSC-1993-49,(1994) 3 Sri LR 245,LKSC,1993,"Exclusive, undisturbed, adverse, and uninterrupted possession of a property for a significant period can prove a claim to have prescribed to that property.","(1) Amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda was possible and the corpus (Batadombagahawatta) did not include lots 5B, 5C, 5D and 6B and should be excluded. (2) The causes of the 3rd defendant and 4th defendant had to be considered separately. The deeds of the 3rd defendant referred to undivided interests in the whole land, while the deed by which' Ratnayake's widow conveyed her interests to the 4th defendant referred to interests in a divided lot. (3) The amicable partition on Plan 1053 with the amalgamation of lot 5 with Kendagahalanda in 1951, significant improvement of lot 5 by planting the entirety of lot 5 (except the paddy portion and a narrow slip adjoining the main road) and the 4th defendant's dealing with lot 5 as a distinct entity along with the continuous exclusive, undisturbed, adverse and uninterrupted possession by herself and her predecessors in title prove her claim to have prescribed to lot 5. (4) The partition should be confined to lots 3, 4 and 7 as shown in the preliminary plan but 4th defendant will not be entitled to any shares. (5) The interests of Tudor, one of the three children of the 3rd defendant, will remain unallotted if the 3rd defendant, on being given an opportunity, fails to prove that these rights passed to her",SRC071-s3,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\rules_cleaned.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKSC-1999-54-1,commonlii-LKSC-1999-54,(1999) 2 Sri LR 4,LKSC,1999,A non-notarial document is of no force or avail in law under section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance.,"P2 being a non-notarial document was of no force or avail in law in view of section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance. However, in a case where fraud is pleaded, put in issue and is established by the evidence on record, it is open to the court to take into consideration such document. Per G. P. S. de Silva, CJ. ''The rigour of the provisions of section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance may, on proof of fraud as in the present case, be relaxed on the principle that 'the Statute of Frauds may not be made an instrument of fraud."" 5",SRC001-s2,none,,,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
