scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_126_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_126_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A claim of Laesio Enormis cannot be maintained if the seller knew the true value of the property at the time of sale.,"Citing relevant judgments pronounced in this regard by our Superior Courts, it was contended that under such circumstances, a claim of Laesio Enormis cannot be maintained.",SRC071-s10,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_128_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_128_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,"If the language of the enactment is clear and unambiguous, it would not be legitimate for the Courts to add words by implication into the language.","If the language of the enactment is clear and unambiguous, it would not be legitimate for the Courts to add words by implication into the language. It is a settled law of interpretation that the words are to be interpreted as they appear in the provision, simple and grammatical meaning is to be given to them, and nothing can be added or subtracted.",SRC071-s9,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_129_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_129_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A dedication of immovable property to the Sangha or a Buddhist temple does not become invalid by the absence of a notarial document.,That a dedication once made is not rendered ineffective by the absence of a notarial document executed in accordance with the Prevention of Fraud Ordinance.,SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_12_2006.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_12_2006.pdf,,SC,2006,The burden of proof lies on the party asserting that the condition of the land has changed.,"When a person is bound to prove the existence of any fact, it is said that the burden of proof lies on that person.",SRC029-s101,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_131_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_131_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,Mere use of a footpath does not establish a servitude of right of way by prescription.,Merely walking on a footpath does not make it an adverse act conflicting with the rights of the owner.,SRC071-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_132_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_132_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A person can acquire land through adverse possession by possessing it openly and as if they were the owner for an uninterrupted period of ten years or more.,"For a comparable analysis, let me refer to the South African position. Section 1 of the Prescription Act, No. 68 of 1969 in South Africa reads as follows: “Subject to the provisions of this Chapter and of Chapter IV, a person shall by prescription become the owner of a thing which he has possessed openly and as if he were the owner thereof for an uninterrupted period of thirty years or for a period which, together with any periods for which such thing was so possessed by his predecessors in title, constitutes an uninterrupted period of 30 years.”",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_135_2023.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_135_2023.pdf,,SC,2023,"A fiscal's conveyance is not required for title to pass in a fiscal sale, and possession by the purchaser for the prescriptive period establishes title by prescription.","If a fiscal’s conveyance is not executed in favour of the purchaser of a land at a fiscal sale, no title passes. If, however, the purchaser enters into possession after the sale and possessed the land for longer than the prescriptive period, title by prescription is established.",SRC071-s13,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_139_2022.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_139_2022.pdf,,SC,2022,The true nature of the contracts in substance is that they are for the uprooting and removal of trees for the supply of rubber logs.,The true nature of the contracts in substance is that they are for the uprooting and removal of trees for the supply of rubber logs.,SRC002-s7,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_144_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"Proof of prescriptive title against co-owners is more difficult. Unless the contrary is proved by cogent evidence, possession of one co-owner is in law the possession of the other co-owners.","Unless the contrary is proved by cogent evidence, possession of one co-owner is in law the possession of the other co-owners. (Corea v. Iseris Appuhamy (1911) 15 NLR 65, Sediris Appuhamy v. James Appuhamy (1958) 60 NLR 297 at 302-303, Rajapaksha v. Hendrick Singho (1959) 61 NLR 32, Maria Perera v. Albert Perera [1983] 2 Sri LR 399, Wickremaratne and Another v. Alpenis Perera [1986] 1 Sri LR 190 at 195, Maria Fernando v. Anthony Fernando [1997] 2 Sri LR 356)",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_150_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_150_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A Director of a corporate entity who mortgages his property as security for loans obtained by that corporate entity is a borrower within the meaning of the Recovery of Loans by Banks (Special Provisions) Act No.4 of 1990.,The borrower is none other than the person to whom a loan is granted and who is required in terms of Section 2 to register his address with the Bank.,SRC004-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The burden of proof lies on the party who wishes the court to believe in the existence of a fact.,"In terms of Section 103, since the Appellant has raised issues disputing that P 1 and P 2 is forgery he is the person who wishes the court to believe the existence of that fact. Hence the burden of proof lies on the Appellant to prove the existence of a forgery.",SRC029-s103,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A co-owner's possession is in law the possession of all the other co-owners.,Every co-owner is presumed to be in possession in his capacity as a co-owner.,SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2023.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_151_2023.pdf,,SC,2023,"A party asserting prescriptive title must discharge the burden of proof to establish uninterrupted, undisturbed, and adverse possession.","In the absence of such proof, the burden must be held not to have been discharged.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_155_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_155_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The occupation of the premises must be of such character as is incompatible with the title of the owner.,The occupation of the premises must be of such character as is incompatible with the title of the owner.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_155_2022.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_155_2022.pdf,,SC,2022,A plea of prescription requires merely ten years of uninterrupted and undisturbed possession.,"Proof of the undisturbed and uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of lands or immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff in such action (that is to say, a possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce, or performance of service or duty, or by any other act by the possessor, from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred) for ten years previous to the bringing of such action, shall entitle the defendant to a decree in his favour with costs...",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_159_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_159_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,"To claim prescriptive title, a party must prove possession by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff, with a specific starting point and documentary evidence.",Where a party invokes the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property the burden of proof rests fairly and squarely on him to establish the starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights [Gratiean J in Chelliah v Wijenathan 54 N.L.R. 337 at 342].,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_15_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A court may presume the existence of any fact which it thinks likely to have happened, regard being had to the common course of natural events, human conduct, and public and private business in their relation to the facts of the particular case – that judicial and official acts have been regularly performed.",This evidence contained in the affidavit has not been challenged either by raising an issue on this matter or calling the Fiscal Officer who executed the writ and eliciting the fact that possession had not been handed over as claimed by the Appellant. No independent evidence was led to rebut this presumption.,SRC029-s114(d),bad-source,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_160_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_160_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,"Parole evidence can be used to prove a constructive trust, even if the transferor did not intend to pass the beneficial interest in the property.",The Prevention of Frauds Ordinance and Sec. 92 of the Evidence Ordinance do not bar parole evidence to prove a constructive trust and that the transferor did not intend to pass the beneficial interest in the property.,SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_167_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_167_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,"Parole evidence can be led to prove that there was no agreement which was intended to be so, contained in the document.","Parole evidence can be led to prove that there was no agreement which was intended to be so, contained in the document.",SRC001-s92,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_169_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_169_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A party may lead evidence of an informal promise to reconvey to establish a trust governed by Section 5(3) or Chapter IX of the Trust Ordinance.,"If not, as has been previously observed by this Court, said provisions of the Trust Ordinance effectively becomes nugatory.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_170_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_170_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A donee's failure to provide financial assistance to a donor, especially when the donor is in need and has no other means of support, can be considered as 'other equally grave causes' of gross ingratitude.","The donee Defendant has failed to do so. She could not have acted any worse than turning a blind eye to someone who did not ask for any assistance monthly from 1992 to 2005, when asked for financial assistance of a small amount compared to the earning capacity of someone living in U.K. There are numerous ways she could have helped if she really cared to do so but she never did.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_171_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_171_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"In a possessory action, the plaintiff need not prove lawful title to the subject matter of the action.",It is absolutely clear that a possessory action can be instituted without proof of title. The Plaintiff in a possessory action need not prove at all that he has lawful title to the subject matter of the action.,SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance and Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance do not bar parole evidence to prove a constructive trust and that the transferor did not intend to pass the beneficial interest in the property.,"I am in respectful agreement with a view expressed by His Lordship Justice Dheeraratne. After considering the above legal literature, I would like to follow the principle laid down in the case of Dayawathi and Others Vs. Gunasekera and Another (Supra) and I hold that Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance and Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance do not operate as a bar to lead parole evidence to prove a constructive trust and to prove that the transferor did not intend to dispose of beneficial interest in the property.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,A document executed in terms of Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance is valid.,"The Document marked P2 which is the Agreement to Sell entered before Lakshmi Surige, AAL and Notary Public is a document executed in terms of Section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2025.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_175_2025.pdf,,SC,2025,A deed executed before a notary can be proved by the notary if they knew the executant personally and can testify that the signature on the deed is that of the executant.,"Where a deed executed before a notary is sought to be proved, the notary can be regarded as an attesting witness within the meaning of section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance if the notary knew the executant personally and can testify that the signature on the deed is that of the executant.",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_178_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_178_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A party cannot adduce oral evidence against the contents of a notarially executed deed to prove fraud and/or duress/intimidation, unless the fact may be proved which will invalidate any document on the basis of fraud, intimidation, illegality, want of consideration etc.","Under section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance oral evidence could not be led which is inconsistent with the contents of the deed. Under proviso (1) to section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance any fact may be proved which will invalidate any document on the basis of fraud, intimidation, illegality, want of consideration etc.",SRC029-s92,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_181_182_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_181_182_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A person's possession of land can be considered adverse to another's interest if it continues for a long time without interruption.,"The cumulative effect of the evidence of the Plaintiff supported by the evidence given by Grama Niladhari Gama Ethige Don Francis Lambert is a clear establishment before Court that the nature of the possession of the relevant land maintained and enjoyed by the 2nd, 3rd and the 4th Defendants, was a possession which was adverse to the interest of both the Plaintiff and Gnana Jayasinghe.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_189_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_189_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,The certificate of sale is conclusive proof in respect of that property and as regards its sale being duly complied with in terms of the Act.,"As such the certificate of sale cannot be challenged, if and when it is issued in terms of the said Act.",SRC004-s15,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_194_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"To claim prescriptive title under Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance, the claimant’s possession to the property should be an adverse possession or independent possession.","To claim prescriptive title under Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance, the claimant’s possession to the property should be an adverse possession or independent possession.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_197_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_197_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A party claiming a declaration of title must have title themselves.,"There is abundant authority that, a party claiming a declaration of title must have title himself. ... The authorities unite in holding that plaintiff must show title to the corpus in dispute and that if he cannot, the action will not lie.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_198_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_198_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,The probative value of a death certificate is limited to the details of the birth or death or still-birth to which it refers.,The probative value of the contents of a death certificate issued under both Ordinance No. 1 of 1895 and Births and Deaths Registration Act No. 17 of 1951 is limited in terms of those two enactments to the details of the birth or death or still-birth to which it refers and applies to.,SRC029-s21,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_1_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_1_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A document marked 'P3' without objection at the closure of the case is deemed to be admitted as evidence.,"Therefore, at this juncture, the document marked ‘P3’ ought to be admitted as evidence for all purposes of law considering the fact that no objection was taken at the closure of the Plaintiffs-Appellants case.",SRC029-s114,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
