scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_218_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_218_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,Formal admissions recorded at the trial need no further proof unless the Court wants them to be proved.,"58. No fact need be proved in any proceeding which the parties thereto or their agents agree to admit at the hearing, or which, before the hearing, they agree to admit by any writing under their hands, or which by any rule of pleading in force at the time they are deemed to have admitted by their pleadings: Provided that the court may, in its discretion, require the facts admitted to be proved otherwise than by such admissions.",SRC029-s31,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_21_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_21_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A claim for the price of goods sold and delivered on an unwritten contract falls within section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance.,An action “for or in respect of any goods sold and delivered” will be prescribed by a period of one year even if it is based on an unwritten contract between the parties to the action in relation to such “goods sold and delivered”.,SRC001-s7,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_225_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_225_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A lessee or licensee cannot question the title of the person who gave them the lease or licence, and must first quit the land before challenging the title.","No tenant of immovable property, or person claiming through such tenant, shall during the continuance of the tenancy, be permitted to deny that the landlord of such tenant had, at the beginning of the tenancy, a title to such immovable property; and no person who came upon any immovable property by the licence of the person in possession thereof shall be permitted to deny that such person had a title to such possession at the time when such licence was given.",SRC029-s116,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_231_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_231_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"A deed for the sale or transfer of land, being a document which is required by law to be attested, has to be proved in the manner set out in Section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance by proof that the maker (the vendor) of that document signed it in the presence of witnesses and notary.","A deed for the sale or transfer of land, being a document which is required by law to be attested, has to be proved in the manner set out in Section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance by proof that the maker (the vendor) of that document signed it in the presence of witnesses and notary.",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_232_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_232_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A licensee cannot claim prescriptive title against the owner or their heirs on the basis of the period they possessed the property.,"When a person starts possessing an immovable property with leave and licence of the owner, the presumption is that he continues to possess the immovable property on the permission originally granted and such a person or his agents or heirs cannot claim prescriptive title against the owner or his heirs on the basis of the period he possessed the property.",SRC029-s116,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_235_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_235_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"In order to succeed in a claim of prescriptive title, proof of undisturbed and uninterrupted possession, under a title adverse to or independent of that of the owner of the immovable property, for a period of ten years is required.","Proof of the undisturbed and uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of lands or immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff in such action (that is to say, a possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce, or performance of service or duty, or by any other act by the possessor, from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred) for ten years previous to the bringing of such action, shall entitle the defendant to a decree in his favour with costs.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_242_2016_amd1-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_242_2016_amd1-1.pdf,,SC,2016,A person cannot acquire prescriptive right to a property unless they produce an event or an overt act that manifests their intention to occupy the property as the owner.,the Respondent (Defendant) cannot acquire prescriptive right to the subject property unless he produces of an event or an overt act that manifest her intention to occupy the property as the owner as against the Petitioner (Appellant).,SRC071-s13,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_27_2023.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_27_2023.pdf,,SC,2023,"The standard of proof required for establishing a right of way under Section 69(2) of the Primary Courts' Procedure Act is 'actual use', not 'entitlement to use'.","It is sufficient, for the purpose of issuing such a declaration, that there is evidence of ‘actual use’ to substantiate one’s entitlement.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,The burden of proving prescriptive right to a distinct and separate land is with the party claiming it.,once the paper title to the subject matter of this action is proved to be with the Plaintiff and the 1st Defendant the burden of proving the prescriptive right to the subject matter of this action as a distinct and separate land within the meaning of 5 SC/APPEAL/28/2014 section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance is with the 3rd Defendant which the 3rd Defendant has failed to do even on the finding of the Learned trial Judge as to the possession of the subject matter of this action?,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_28_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,"The possession of a property by a defendant can be considered 'undisturbed and uninterrupted possession' if they have been in continuous possession for a period of time, as established by the Prescription Ordinance.","In the presence of the above evidence, it is not difficult to hold that the possession of this property by Defendant is ‘undisturbed and uninterrupted possession’ within the meaning of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_29_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_29_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,"A creditor who entered into a settlement in a Debt Conciliation Board in which a conditional transfer figured, is not prevented from later instituting a vindicatory action claiming title based on that conditional transfer.",I do not see that there can be any disability for the plaintiff to bring an action upon the title he had obtained by the deed of transfer in his favour upon the footing that there had been a default resulting in the right to redeem having come to an end.,SRC001-s43,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_29_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_29_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,"A deed is considered duly attested if it is signed by the executant, the notary, and the witnesses at the same time, in the presence of one another.",An instrument which is required by section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance to be notorially attested must be signed by the notary and the witnesses at the same time as the maker and in his presence.,SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_30_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_30_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,Gross ingratitude should be proved with certainty and with sound evidence.,If a party is able to prove gross ingratitude would deprive a person of a property right.,SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_30_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_30_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A person asserting adverse possession has no equities in his favour. He alone must prove prescriptive title with cogent evidence.,A person asserting adverse possession has no equities in his favour. He alone must prove prescriptive title with cogent evidence.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_32_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_32_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,A party cannot take up new positions which are questions of fact for the first time on appeal.,The defendants cannot take up new positions which are questions of fact for the first time on appeal.,SRC029-s114,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2009-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2009-1.pdf,,SC,2009,The positive rule of law in Section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance applies when an action is filed after a lapse of one year after the date of the last invoice.,"In the circumstances, it is clear that the positive rule of law referred to in Section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance shall apply in this instance.",SRC071-s8,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_33_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A person cannot acquire prescriptive title to property without demonstrating an overt act showing an intention to possess adversely to the owner.,"Without any demonstration of any overt act against the rights of the owner, court cannot recognize Nandasekera as a person who has acted as one holding prescriptive possession against the owner who had given him leave and license to step in and live there, no matter how long he had enjoyed the property.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_34_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_34_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A person who strikes another with a formidable weapon must be presumed to have known that the act is imminently dangerous to the life of the person at whom he strikes.,Thus a man who strikes at the back of another a violent blow with a formidable weapon must be taken to know that he is doing an act imminently dangerous to the life of the person at whom he strikes and that a probable result of his act will be to cause that person’s death.,SRC029-s6,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_-2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_-2018.pdf,,SC,2018,"Unless overridden by a significant personal law, the Matrimonial Rights and Inheritance Ordinance, No. 15 of 1876 generally applies to matters pertaining to intestate succession.","However, if one were to consider the applicability of the Muslim Intestate Succession Ordinance, No. 10 of 1931, it is imperative that it be applied subject to appropriate construction as to its provisions with regard to whom and in what respect it would apply.",SRC004,ok,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"If no objection is made to a document being marked when it was first tendered in evidence, it becomes evidence for all the purposes of the case and it cannot be challenged only in appeal.","Hence, if no objection is made to a document being marked when it was first tendered in evidence, it becomes evidence for all the purposes of the case and it cannot be challenged only in appeal.",SRC029-s154,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_36_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,The burden of proof rests fairly and squarely on the party invoking the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription to establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"Where a party invokes the provisions of Section 3 of the Prescription, the burden of proof rests fairly and squarely on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights [Chelliah v. Wijenathan et al (54 N.L.R. 337)].",SRC001-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_38_2014-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_38_2014-1.pdf,,SC,2014,"A presumption arises that a Last Will has been destroyed by the Testator when the Last Will can be traced back to the Testator at the time of death, but cannot be found after the death.","Where the Last Will can be traced to the Testator and where the Last Will cannot be found with the Testator at the time of death, the presumption arises that the Last Will has been destroyed.",SRC001-s6,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_39_2024.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_39_2024.pdf,,SC,2024,A successful action for declaration of title interrupts prescription.,A successful action for declaration of title is an interruption of possession.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"The word 'owner' in Section 40 of the Forest Ordinance should be given a purposive interpretation, considering the intention of the legislature.",In my view the word “owner” as it occurs in Section 40 of the Forest Ordinance cannot be considered in isolation applying purely legal definition of the term “owner” but must be given a purposive interpretation taking into account the intention of the legislature.,SRC001-s40,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A licensee cannot challenge the title of the owner of the property in suit.,"A licencee as the Defendant who entered the property in dispute with the leave and licence of Plaintiff cannot in law challenge the title of the owner of the property in suit, in this case the Plaintiff party.",SRC029-s116,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2024.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_43_2024.pdf,,SC,2024,The jurisdiction to enforce an arbitral award is distinct and different from the jurisdiction to set aside an award.,The jurisdiction that the High Court exercises in such application is limited to determine whether the award should be enforced.,SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_45_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_45_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,"A document required to be attested cannot be used as evidence unless its genuineness has been either admitted or established by proof, which should be given before the document is accepted by Court.","A document cannot be used in evidence, unless its genuineness has been either admitted or established by proof, which should be given before the document is accepted by Court.",SRC001-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_45_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_45_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,"A claim of prescription cannot lie in respect of an undivided share, but only in respect of a definite and identifiable portion of land.","In any event, it is well settled that a claim of prescription cannot lie in respect of an undivided share, but only in respect of a definite and identifiable portion of land.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_48_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_48_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A notary cannot give evidence to support a disputed document without proper proof.,"will the named Notary (in P2 & P3) take it upon himself to give evidence and support the Defendant-Appellant-Petitioner’s case as far as V1 is concerned in breach of 11 the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance, Stamp Duty Ordinance, and the Notaries Ordinance?",SRC001-s11,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_48_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_48_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A servitude may be acquired either by express grant or by prescription.,A servitude may be acquired either by express grant or by prescription.,SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2007.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2007.pdf,,SC,2007,An acknowledgment of liability in writing can take a cause of action out of the limitation period imposed by the Prescription Ordinance.,"If so, in any event, is the cause of action of the Plaintiff-Appellants set out in the Plaint, taken out from the limitation imposed in terms of Section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance?",SRC071-s8,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,The court cannot permit the addition of a party after the prescriptive period.,The District Court could not permit the Plaintiff to add a party after the prescriptive period.,SRC071-s9,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_4_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,A contract may be avoided by Court on the ground of laesio enormis either when the purchaser pays more than double the true value of the thing or the vendor sells the thing for less than half its value.,A contract may be avoided by Court on the ground of laesio enormis either when the purchaser pays more than double the true value of the thing or the vendor sells the thing for less than half its value.,SRC001-s2,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_51_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_51_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,A contract requires an offer and acceptance.,"every contract requires an offer and acceptance. Therefore an offer or promise which is not accepted, is not actionable [vide Justice Weerasooriya in Muthukuda v. Sumanatwathie, (1962) 65 NLR 205, 208, 209].",SRC029-s116,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_51_2020-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_51_2020-1.pdf,,SC,2020,Courts may countenance infirmities in proving original ownership of land if it is a realistic approach rather than a legalistic one.,"Courts by and large countenance infirmities in this regard, if infirmities they be, in an approach which is realistic rather than legalistic, as to do otherwise would be to put the relief given by partition decrees outside the reach of very many persons seeking to end the co-ownership.",SRC029-s32(5),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_52_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_52_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,"An employer cannot transfer an employee from one company to another, whether it is an associate company or a subsidiary company, merely because the directors were the same.","The Court went onto observe, if the employer relying upon a term of contract which permit transfer of employees from one section or from one department to another, make use of such a course of action to move employees from one legal entity to another, it would offend the provisions of the Termination of Workmen Act.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,"A co-owner cannot acquire prescriptive title to undivided land, and a partition among co-owners terminates co-ownership only after undisturbed, uninterrupted, and exclusive possession of the divided lots for a period of over ten years.","Where a land is divided with the consent of all the co-owners but no cross conveyances are executed in respect of the lots, co-ownership terminates only after undisturbed, uninterrupted and exclusive possession of the divided lots for a period of over ten years",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A bank cannot sell a property mortgaged by public auction if the original amount of the loan granted was less than Rs.5 million.,"If the original amount of the loan granted was less than Rs.5 million, the bank cannot, in a case of default, sell the property mortgaged by public auction;",SRC004-s1,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_53_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A party invoking the provisions of section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance must establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights with consistent and credible evidence.,"In order to defeat the ownership of an adverse claimant to immovable property, the burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on him to establish a starting point for his or her acquisition of prescriptive rights (Chelliah vs. Wijenathan 54 NLR 337, Solomon Dias vs. William Singho and others [2015] 1 SLR 277).",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_54_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_54_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"A bank is entitled to sell a mortgaged property to recover the total amount due on multiple loans granted to a borrower, even if the borrower has not defaulted on one of the loans.","the foregoing provisions of this Act shall apply notwithstanding that default may not been made in respect of the other loan or any of the other loans and the Board may, in any such case, by resolution under section 4 authorise the sale of the property for the recovery of the total amount due to the Bank in respect of both or all of the loans, as the case may be, and these provisions shall apply accordingly.",SRC004-s12,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_54_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_54_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,A defence of prescription must be specially pleaded by way of defence and cannot be raised for the first time in appeal.,"Basnayaka CJ, in Brampy Appuhamy v Gunasekera (supra) has held that (at p. 255); “[I]t is settled law that when, as in the case of Sections 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10 and 11 of the Prescription Ordinance, the effect of the statute is merely to limit the time in which an action may be brought and not to extinguish the right, the Court will not take the statute into account unless it is specially pleaded by way of defence”.",SRC071-s5,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_54a_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_54a_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A plaintiff must adduce adequate and reliable evidence to establish prescriptive title, including assertive evidence of adverse possession as against mere evidence of occupation.","However, the evidence of the defendant and her son does not suggest that they did any such thing. In this connection, the defendant does not claim that she made any statement to the plaintiff‟s father that she does not accept his title. The defendant does not claim that, after the plaintiff‟s father obtained title, she made any alterations to the property or paid the rates and taxes in respect of the property in her name or obtained electricity and water connections in her name. The defendant has not led the evidence of the Grama Sevaka or her neighbours to suggest that she was considered to be holding the property in her own right after the property was transferred to the plaintiff‟s father in 1978.",SRC071-s13,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,"A judge must consider all prayers in a plaint, even in an ex parte trial.","Even in an ex parte trial, the judge must act according to law and ensure that the relief claimed is due in fact and in law, and must dismiss the plaintiff's claim if he is not entitled to it. An ex parte judgment cannot be entered without a hearing and an adjudication.",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_55_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,A party must prove the due execution of a document to establish its validity.,"The learned judge has noted that both the 2nd Defendant and the Plaintiff have not discharged the burden cast on them by section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance read with section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance, which required them to prove that the two documents",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_56_2023.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_56_2023.pdf,,SC,2023,A plea of prescription requires ten years of uninterrupted and undisturbed possession.,"Proof of the undisturbed and uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of lands or immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff in such action (that is to say, a possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce, or performance of service or duty, or by any other act by the possessor, from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred) for ten years previous to the bringing of such action, shall entitle the defendant to a decree in his favour with costs...",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_58_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_58_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,A person who possesses a land with secret intention cannot claim that his possession is an adverse possession.,A person who possesses a land with secret intention cannot claim that his possession is an adverse possession. Possession of a land by a person with secret intention cannot be considered as an adverse possession in terms of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_59_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_59_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,A court must consider the relevance of evidence before admitting it.,"Evidence may be given in any suit or proceeding of the existence or non-existence of every fact in issue, and of such other facts as are hereinafter declared to be relevant and of no others.",SRC029-s5,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_5_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_5_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,"A party claiming prescription must prove the commencement of adverse possession, which includes an act of ouster, prior to the running of prescription.",8 unless a party is able to prove that there had been an act of ouster prior to the running of prescription.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_60_2004.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_60_2004.pdf,,SC,2004,The failure to challenge evidence by cross-examination may not be sufficient to hold that a particular fact had been proved within the meaning of Section 3 of the Evidence Ordinance.,"It might, however, be a factor to be taken into account in accepting such evidence.",SRC029-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_61_2013-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_61_2013-1.pdf,,SC,2013,A party claiming prescriptive title to a land must identify the land with definite boundaries.,One cannot claim prescription to an unidentified or unknown land.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_62_2024.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_62_2024.pdf,,SC,2024,"A defendant who executes a promissory note to address a defect in title and possession remains bound by the express conditions in the note, even if the conveyance is disputed.",No adequate evidence was adduced at trial to establish that ‘Pa 3’ was signed under duress or coercion.,SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_63_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_63_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,A document cannot be admitted into evidence until its genuineness has either been admitted by the opposing party or established through proof.,"The judicial approach to authenticity was clearly articulated in the case of Robins v Grogan [43 N.L.R. 269 at 270], where the Court held that a document cannot be admitted into evidence until its genuineness has either been admitted by the opposing party or established through proof.",SRC029-s67,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_66_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_66_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,"A record in writing, signed by the owner of the land, renouncing all claims to compensation, and signed in the presence of the Chairman or a person authorized by the Chairman, is sufficient to vest the land in the Council.",He must duly sign such record by himself. 4. The signing must be done in the presence of the Chairman or of a person authorized by the Chairman in writing in that behalf.,SRC042-s52,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_68_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_68_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A settlement between a plaintiff and a defendant in a previous rei vindicatio action cannot bind a third party who acquired rights from the vendor.,The settlement cannot bind the vendee Nalika Jeewanthi as she was not a party to the settlement.,SRC004-s35,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_69_2019-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_69_2019-1.pdf,,SC,2019,A party claiming prescriptive possession must provide clear and cogent evidence of an overt act marking the commencement of adverse possession.,"Moreover, our courts have consistently held that, where a co-owner claims prescriptive title, there must be clear and cogent evidence of an overt act marking the commencement of adverse possession.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_69_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_69_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,A licensee cannot claim prescriptive title to a property without an overt act demonstrating adverse possession.,"A person who is let into occupation of property as a tenant or as a licensee must be deemed to continue to occupy on the footing on which he was admitted, until by some overt act he manifests his intention of occupying in another capacity.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,An appellate court should be slow to interfere with a trial judge's decision on facts and circumstances of a case.,"Hence, they become the best judges as to the facts of the case and His Lordship with approval referred to the observation made by Justice G.P.S.de Silva (as he then was) in the case of Alwis v. Piyasena Fernando 1993 1 SLR 111",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A co-owner may sue another co-owner of an adjoining land for the definition of boundaries without the participation of other co-owners.,"It is a well-established principle of law that a co-owner could sue a trespasser without the participation of other co-owners. (Hevawitrane v. Dangan Rubber Co., Ltd. 17 NLR 49; Arnolisa et al v. Dissan et al 4 NLR 163; Meera Lebbe Cassi Lebbe Marikkar v. Kalavilage Baba (1885) 7 S.C.C.) If a co-owner could sue a trespasser, in the same way a co-owner should be able to be sued without participation of other co-owners.",SRC029-s115,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_74_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_74_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A subsequent purchase of title by a party claiming prescription to the same land does not negate the prescriptive title acquired by such party to such land.,"any subsequent purchase of title by a party claiming prescription to the same land, does not negate the prescriptive title acquired by such party to such land.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_75_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_75_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A party does not need to show any other right to use a road if it is part of the subject matter in a previous partition action.,"Hence, the Plaintiff has every right to use this road as part of the subject matter in that partition action.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_77_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_77_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A party's prescriptive title to a defined portion of land can be established by showing possession of that portion for a period of over 60 years.,"the Defendant-Appellant contends inter alia, that she and her predecessors in title possessed the unallotted Lot No. 3 in extent of 2R and 8.20P, depicted in Preliminary Plan No. 1175/P, dated 30/07/1998, made by A.C.P. Gunasena, Licensed Surveyor, for a period of over 60 years and thereby acquired prescriptive title to the said Lot No.3 in the said Plan No. 1175/P.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_78_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_78_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A mere statement of a witness of occupying a land for long years would not suffice to satisfy the requirements of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,A mere statement of a witness of occupying a land for long years would not suffice to satisfy the requirements of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_7_2004.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_7_2004.pdf,,SC,2004,At least one of the two attesting witnesses should also be called to prove the due execution of a deed when the execution is challenged and at least one of the attesting witnesses is alive.,"where the execution of a deed is challenged on the ground that it had been signed before it was written, then, where at least one of the two attesting witnesses is alive, the evidence of the notary alone, even where he knew the executant, is not sufficient; at least one of the two attesting witnesses should also be called.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_7_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_7_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,"A person can acquire prescriptive title to land by possessing it for a long time, even if they do not claim it as part of a larger landholding.",There is no doubt that the 4th Defendant has prescribed to Lots D and E by a title adverse to or independent of that of the Plaintiff and the 1st to 3rd Defendants as required by section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_80_2022.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_80_2022.pdf,,SC,2022,Adverse possession commences from the moment the defendant intends to possess the land of the plaintiff as if he were the owner of it.,"There must be a corporeal occupation of land attended with a manifest intention to hold and continue it and when the intent plainly is to hold the land against the claim of all other persons, the possession is hostile or adverse to the rights of the true owner",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_82_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_82_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,The burden of proof lies on the person who would fail if no evidence at all were given on either side.,Section 102 – “The burden of proof in a suit or proceeding lies on that person who would fail if no evidence at all were given on either side.”,SRC029-s102,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_83_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_83_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,Dispossession may be by force or by not allowing the possessor to use at his discretion what he possesses.,"Dispossession may be by force or by not allowing the possessor to use at his discretion what he possesses whether it is done by sowing, or ploughing or by building or repairing something or by doing anything at all by which they do not leave the free possession of the person who was dispossessed.",SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_92_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_92_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A tenant cannot deny the landlord's title to the property during the continuance of the tenancy.,"No tenant of immovable property, or person claiming through such tenant, shall during the continuance of the tenancy, be permitted to deny that the landlord of such tenant had, at the beginning of the tenancy, a title to such immovable property;",SRC029-s116,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_93_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_93_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A party is not entitled to plead legal or paper title and prescriptive title together in the same action.,a party is not entitled to plead legal or paper title and prescriptive title together in the same action,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_93_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_93_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,"A party cannot invoke the extraordinary jurisdiction of revision as of right, and must explain in the petition why they did not exercise their right of appeal.","When a right of appeal is available against a Judgment or an Order, a party seeking to come before Court by way of revision shall explain in the petition why he did not exercise his right of appeal.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_94_2013-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_94_2013-1.pdf,,SC,2013,A possessory action under Section 4 of the Prescription Ordinance does not apply when the plaintiff's case is based on the terms and conditions of a lease agreement.,"Therefore, merely because the reliefs sought in the plaint are to restore him in possession, it cannot change the character and the scope of the plaint particularly when arguing an appeal.",SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_94_2025.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_94_2025.pdf,,SC,2025,"To establish prescriptive acquisition of immovable property, a defendant must prove undisturbed and uninterrupted possession for 10 years.","Proof of the undisturbed and uninterrupted possession by a defendant in any action, or by those under whom he claims, of lands or immovable property, by a title adverse to or independent of that of the claimant or plaintiff in such action (that is to say, a possession unaccompanied by payment of rent or produce, or performance of service or duty, or by any other act by the possessor, from which an acknowledgment of a right existing in another person would fairly and naturally be inferred) for ten years previous to the bringing of such action, shall entitle the defendant to a decree in his favour with costs.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_94_and_95_2019.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_94_and_95_2019.pdf,,SC,2019,"A contract of employment entered into with a defaulting bank will be deemed to be a contract entered into with the acquiring bank, with effect from the date of vesting.","a) all contracts, deeds, bonds, agreements, powers of attorney, grants of legal representation and other instruments of whatever nature pertaining or relating to the vested business of the defaulting bank, subsisting or having effect on the day immediately preceding the relevant date, to which the defaulting bank is a party or which are in favour of the defaulting bank, shall be deemed with effect from the relevant date to be contracts, deeds, bonds, agreements, powers of attorney, grants of legal representation or other instruments entered into or granted, as the case may be, by the acquiring bank;",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_95_2018.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_95_2018.pdf,,SC,2018,Uncontradicted evidence adduced by a party is a fact in favour of that party's case.,"In other words, uncontradicted evidence adduced by the 2nd Defendant in the trial is a fact in favour of the 2nd Defendant’s case.",SRC029-s03,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_96_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_96_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,"A period of prescription can be 'tacked' onto another period, and the progress of prescription is not arrested by a disability such as minority or absence beyond seas.","As far as this case is concerned, the period from 1954 to 1962 [7 years] can be ‘tacked’ onto the period from 1979 to 1984 [6 years] in computing the period the Defendants were in possession for the purpose of term of prescription and regarding the application of the proviso to Section 3 of the Ordinance the progress of prescription is not arrested subject, however, to discounting the period Rosa enjoyed life interest to the corpus.",SRC071-s13,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_97_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_97_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A possessory action presupposes the existence of possession ut dominus and a dispossession otherwise than by process of law.,A possessory action presupposes the existence of possession ut dominus and a dispossession otherwise than by process of law.,SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A notary who attests a document in terms of Prevention of Frauds Ordinance is generally competent to testify under section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance.,A notary who attests a deed is an attesting witness within the meaning of that expression in section 68 and section 69 of the Evidence Ordinance.,SRC001-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,"Once paper title becomes undisputed, the burden shifts to the defendants to show that they have independent rights in the form of prescription as claimed by them.",Once paper title became undisputed the burden shifted to the defendants to show that they had independent rights in the form of prescription as claimed by them.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_16_2007.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_16_2007.pdf,,SC,2007,The President's belief in the necessity or expediency of an emergency regulation is deemed conclusive of its validity.,"As established in the case of Yasapala, the President's belief in the necessity or expediency of an emergency regulation is deemed conclusive of its validity, as he is not bound as a matter of law to disclose the reasons for the proclamation.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_19_2013-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_19_2013-1.pdf,,SC,2013,A party may lead evidence aliunde to controvert the consideration stated in a deed.,"By reason of proviso (1) to Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance, a party is not per se precluded from leading evidence aliunde to controvert the consideration stated in a deed.",SRC029-s92,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_1_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_1_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A party cannot recover a debt if the amount due has prescribed under the Prescription Ordinance.,"Focusing his attention on the sums of money purportedly due to the Plaintiff from the Defendant on account of the ‘running account’ (“P5” to “P23”), the learned Judge has noted that the amount due per each invoice had become prescribed (as per section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance) as a result of action not having been filed within 1 year of the last payment due.",SRC071-s8,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_23_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_23_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"The prescription period should be calculated from the date of the alleged cause of action and the institution of the action in the District Court, not from the date on which the case was transferred to the Commercial High Court.","Hence, in computing the prescription period, it should be calculated from the date of the alleged cause of action and the institution of the action in the District Court and not from the date on which the case was transferred to the Commercial High Court.",SRC071-s6,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_24_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_24_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A contract of suretyship is in essence a contract by which one person, the surety, agrees to answer for some existing or future liability of another, the principal (or principal debtor), to a third party, the creditor, and by which the surety’s liability is in addition to, and not in substitution for, the liability of the principal.","A contract of suretyship is in essence a contract by which one person, the surety, agrees to answer for some existing or future liability of another, the principal (or principal debtor), to a third party, the creditor, and by which the surety’s liability is in addition to, and not in substitution for, the liability of the principal.",SRC029-s101,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_29_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_29_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,An unwritten promise or agreement between parties is enforceable if the action is commenced within three years from the time the cause of action arose.,"I hold according to the evidence placed by documents before court that there was an unwritten promise/bargain between the parties with regard to payments for the advertisements and programs telecast after 2004 December. The cause of action to recover the dues had arisen in June, 2005 and action had been filed in September, 2006 which is within 3 years from the date of the cause of action.",SRC071-s7,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_35_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_35_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,A cause of action based on a written promise or contract is not prescribed in law if the action is brought within six years from the date of the breach of such promise or contract.,"Before it could be said that the action falls under section 6 of the Ordinance, it must be shown that the action is based upon a written promise or contract.",SRC071-s6,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_3_2009.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_3_2009.pdf,,SC,2009,An action based on a written contract is not prescribed if brought within six years from the date of the breach of such contract.,"Section 6 of the Prescription Ordinance provides that; “No action shall be maintainable upon ... any written promise, contract, bargain, or agreement, ... unless such action shall be brought within six years from the date of the breach of such ... contract, bargain or agreement, ... from the date when such note or bill shall have become due, or of the last payment of interest thereon.”",SRC071-s6,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_69_2013-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_69_2013-1.pdf,,SC,2013,A document required by law to be attested must have at least one attesting witness called to prove its execution.,"According to that section, such document shall not be used as evidence until one attesting witness at least has been called for the purpose of proving its execution, if there is an attesting witness alive, and subject to the process of the court and capable of giving evidence.",SRC029-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_7_2012.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_7_2012.pdf,,SC,2012,An action to recover a debt based on an account stated is not prescribed within three years.,An action to recover the balance was prescribed in three years.,SRC071-s7,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_fr_13_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_fr_13_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,"A person who has agreed to purchase a house on an installment basis can be considered as having a main document in proof of residence, and is entitled to obtain full marks for that document.","When considering the totality of the evidence placed before this Court, I hold that the 1st Petitioner is entitled to obtain full marks to the document produce marked as 9A, the main document in proof of the Residence of the Petitioner.",SRC001-s4,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_fr_15_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_fr_15_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,The minister must hold an inquiry as he thinks fit before making a decision to forfeit property.,It is a further requirement that the minister should hold such inquiry as he thinks fit before the decision is made.,SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_fr_24_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_fr_24_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,"Public authorities must conform to constitutional requirements, including Article 12, even in the sphere of contracts.","Law in Article 12 of the Constitution includes regulations, rules, directions, principles, guidelines and schemes that are designed to regulate public authorities in their conduct. In the context, whilst Article 12 erects no shield against merely private conduct, public authorities must conform to constitutional requirements , in particular to those set out in Article 12 even in the sphere of contract; and where there is a breach of contract and a violation of the provisions of Article 12 brought about by the same set of facts and circumstances, the aggrieved party cannot be confined to his remedy under the law of contract.",SRC002-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_fr_311_2016-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_fr_311_2016-1.pdf,,SC,2016,A public officer cannot be transferred due to service exigencies at the whims and fancies of a superior.,"Moreover, a public officer cannot be transferred due to service exigencies at the whims and fancies of a superior.",SRC029-s114(f),none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_fr_31_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_fr_31_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,A school can reject an application for admission if the information or documents submitted by the applicant are found to be incorrect or false.,According to the said circular in the event of information or the documents submitted are found to be incorrect or false the 8 interview Board could reject the application,SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_miscellaneous_2_2011.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_miscellaneous_2_2011.pdf,,SC,2011,A gem mining license can be revoked if the licensee has made any incorrect statement or representation on the identity of the corpus or title.,Hence the gem mining licence issued to the Appellant can be revoked should he have made any incorrect statement or representation on the identity of the corpus or title.,SRC001-s7,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1977-4-1,commonlii-LKCA-1977-4,80 NLR 256,LKCA,1977,"That as the evidence and the findings of the Trial Judge show that the assessment numbers given in the respective deeds are the correct assessment numbers of the allotment of land each party intended to buy and in fact purchased and was in possession of, the description of the land by the boundaries was a misdescription which although it would not invalidate the deeds, could be rejected or ignored. The maxim falsa demonstrate non nocet which is embodied in section 95 of the Evidence Ordinance applied. Per Pathirana, J. ""The Assessment numbers provide a definite and sufficient description of the land in the respective deeds of the plaintiff and the defendant of what was intendend to pass and the descriptions in those deeds by boundaries are erroneous"". A PPEAL from the judgment of the District Court of Kandy. H. W. Jayewardene with B, J. Fernando and Miss S. Senaratne for Defendant-Appellant. T. B. Dissanayake for Plaintiff-Respondent","That as the evidence and the findings of the Trial Judge show that the assessment numbers given in the respective deeds are the correct assessment numbers of the allotment of land each party intended to buy and in fact purchased and was in possession of, the description of the land by the boundaries was a misdescription which although it would not invalidate the deeds, could be rejected or ignored. The maxim falsa demonstrate non nocet which is embodied in section 95 of the Evidence Ordinance applied. Per Pathirana, J. ""The Assessment numbers provide a definite and sufficient description of the land in the respective deeds of the plaintiff and the defendant of what was intendend to pass and the descriptions in those deeds by boundaries are erroneous"". A PPEAL from the judgment of the District Court of Kandy. H. W. Jayewardene with B, J. Fernando and Miss S. Senaratne for Defendant-Appellant. T. B. Dissanayake for Plaintiff-Respondent",SRC029-s95,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1984-24-1,commonlii-LKCA-1984-24,(1985) 2 Sri LR 35,LKCA,1984,A party to a suit cannot under s. 3 of the Prescription Ordinance set up the title of a third party who is not his predecessor in title and who has not been joined in the action. The judgment in a case must be declaratory of the right of a party to the suit not of a stranger,A party to a suit cannot under s. 3 of the Prescription Ordinance set up the title of a third party who is not his predecessor in title and who has not been joined in the action. The judgment in a case must be declaratory of the right of a party to the suit not of a stranger,SRC071-s3,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1986-39-1,commonlii-LKCA-1986-39,(1986) 1 Sri LR 378,LKCA,1986,"In a vindicatory suit the plaintiff must prove his title. Having failed to prove his own title, he cannot rely on the weakness of the 1st defendant's title. Whatever the strength of the 1st defendant's case, if the plaintiff fails to establish his title, plaintiff's case must necessarily fail","In a vindicatory suit the plaintiff must prove his title. Having failed to prove his own title, he cannot rely on the weakness of the 1st defendant's title. Whatever the strength of the 1st defendant's case, if the plaintiff fails to establish his title, plaintiff's case must necessarily fail",SRC071-s3,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1988-51-1,commonlii-LKCA-1988-51,(1988) 2 Sri LR 264,LKCA,1988,"The Media on which the plaintiff now seeks a declaration of title are different from. the media on which he, based his title in the earlier case: The cause of action is different","(1) The Media on which the plaintiff now seeks a declaration of title are different from. the media on which he, based his title in the earlier case: The cause of action is different. (2) Prescriptive title has to be re-considered on the basis of the new character in which the plaintiff figures in the second action. Plaintiff would have passing to him whatever title by prescription which Jefferjee had and this differs from the prescriptive title he claimed in the earlier case",SRC071-s3,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1988-51-2,commonlii-LKCA-1988-51,(1988) 2 Sri LR 264,LKCA,1988,Prescriptive title has to be re-considered on the basis of the new character in which the plaintiff figures in the second action. Plaintiff would have passing to him whatever title by prescription which Jefferjee had and this differs from the prescriptive title he claimed in the earlier case,"(1) The Media on which the plaintiff now seeks a declaration of title are different from. the media on which he, based his title in the earlier case: The cause of action is different. (2) Prescriptive title has to be re-considered on the basis of the new character in which the plaintiff figures in the second action. Plaintiff would have passing to him whatever title by prescription which Jefferjee had and this differs from the prescriptive title he claimed in the earlier case",SRC071-s3,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1998-51-1,commonlii-LKCA-1998-51,(1998) 3 Sri LR 46,LKCA,1998,"1. Where a building which is the subject matter is burnt down without the fault of the landlord or tenant, the contract is at an end. 2. The trial Judge on a preponderance of evidence led came to a finding that the plaintiff-appellant had set fire to the house. This is a finding of primary fact by a trial Judge who had seen and heard the witness based upon the credibility of such witness, which is entitled to great weight and utmost consideration. 3. The order of the Rent Board was based on the premise that there existed a building which could be repaired. In the case of a house being let if that is completely burnt, the lease comes to an end, but not where the tenant is able to exercise, many of his rights under the lease notwithstanding the complete destruction of the buildings","1. Where a building which is the subject matter is burnt down without the fault of the landlord or tenant, the contract is at an end. 2. The trial Judge on a preponderance of evidence led came to a finding that the plaintiff-appellant had set fire to the house. This is a finding of primary fact by a trial Judge who had seen and heard the witness based upon the credibility of such witness, which is entitled to great weight and utmost consideration. 3. The order of the Rent Board was based on the premise that there existed a building which could be repaired. In the case of a house being let if that is completely burnt, the lease comes to an end, but not where the tenant is able to exercise, many of his rights under the lease notwithstanding the complete destruction of the buildings",SRC029-s145(1),none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-commonlii-LKCA-1999-83-1,commonlii-LKCA-1999-83,(2000) 3 Sri LR 23,LKCA,1999,A usufructuary mortgage being a devise for use and enjoyment of property in lieu of interest - prescription could run only from a breach of that condition,(1) A usufructuary mortgage being a devise for use and enjoyment of property in lieu of interest - prescription could run only from a breach of that condition. (2) Person who entered property in a subordinate character cannot claim prescriptive rights till he changes his character by an overt act. The proof of adverse possession is a condition precedent to the claim for prescriptive rights. (3) Usufructuary mortgagee is bound to the terms of the Bond itself and his rights are confined to the terms in the bond. He cannot claim prescriptive rights till he changes his character and claim adversely disregarding the conditions of the Bond. There is no question of merger of the entirety of the rights and no prescriptive rights being acquired by the Mortgagee by adverse possession,SRC071-s5,none,headnote,ratio,high,headnote,unverified
