scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dc-785-1998.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dc-785-1998.pdf,,COA,1998,"In a Rei-Vindicatio action, the Plaintiff must prove his title and cannot rely on the weakness of the Defendant's title.","It is settled law that in a Rei-Vindicatio action, the Plaintiff must prove his title in establishing his rights. The Plaintiff cannot rely on the weakness of the Defendant's title.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-dcf-858-1996.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-dcf-858-1996.pdf,,COA,1996,"A person who is let into occupation of property as a licensee must be deemed to continue to occupy on the same footing, and to prove possession, there must be some overt act which manifests their intention to occupy.","A person who is let into occupation of property as a licensee must be deemed to continue to occupy on the same footing. Therefore, in order to prove possession there must be some overt act which manifest his intention to occupying.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a-phc_155_2014.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a-phc_155_2014.pdf,,COA,2014,A party is not entitled in law to claim a servitude of right of way over a road reservation granted by the state.,"The learned Counsel for the Respondent has referred to the case of Aflaflda Saratll Paraflagama v. Dhammadiflna Sarath Paranagama and Others (CA (PHC) APN 11712013 where A. W.A. Salam J. held that; ""Unlike in the case of a dispute relating to possession of immovable property, no time frame has been laid down to the length of time during which the right should have been enjoyed in relation to the purported entitlement. In resolving such a dispute the Judge of the Primary Court is expected to determine as 10 who is entitled to the right which is the subject mailer of the dispute and make an Order under Section 69(2). """,SRC001-s1,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a.-dc_1043_1997.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a.-dc_1043_1997.pdf,,COA,1997,"The burden of seeking and getting evidence before court, in the course of investigation of title to the land sought to be partitioned by parties before Court, prior to deciding what share should go to which party is more the duty of the judge than the contesting parties.","The burden of seeking and getting evidence before court, in the course of investigation of title to the land sought to be partitioned by parties before Court, prior to deciding what share should go to which party is more the duty of the judge than the contesting parties.",SRC029-s110,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a.-dc_657_2000.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a.-dc_657_2000.pdf,,COA,2000,"A court can only investigate title in a partition action within the limits of pleadings, admissions, points of contest, evidence both documentary and oral.","Although there is a duty cast on the court to investigate title in a partition action, the court can do so only within the limits of pleadings, admissions, points of contest, evidence both documentary and oral. The court cannot go on a voyage of discovery tracing the title and finding the shares in the corpus for them [Thilagaratnam v. Athpunathan and Others (1996) 2 Sri.L.R. 66].",SRC029-s90,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-c.a.-dc_769-a-b_1996.pdf-1,courts-coa-c.a.-dc_769-a-b_1996.pdf,,COA,1996,Non-execution of a writ of possession does not prevent the successors of the judgment-creditor from claiming ownership of the land.,"Therefore, I hold that non-execution of the writ of possession does not prevent the successors of G. D. Clarice from claiming the ownership of the said lands by virtue of the judgment in D. C. Panadura Case No. 12417.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-1171-1998-f-18.1.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-1171-1998-f-18.1.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,A person who claims prescriptive title against the rightful owner who has the paper title has a very heavy burden to prove all the requirements prescribed in the Prescription Ordinance.,A person who claims prescriptive title against the rightful owner who has the paper title has a very heavy burden to prove all the requirements prescribed in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
