scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-669-670-2000-f-28.10.2019.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-669-670-2000-f-28.10.2019.pdf,,COA,2019,The person claiming prescriptive title must prove all the ingredients stated in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance to succeed on a claim of prescription.,The person claiming prescriptive title shall prove all the ingredients stated in section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance in order to succeed on a claim of prescription.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-dcf-447-2000-18.9.2020.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-dcf-447-2000-18.9.2020.pdf,,COA,2020,"In a rei vindicatio action, the burden is fairly and squarely on the plaintiff to prove title to the land in the manner pleaded in the plaint.","In a rei vindicatio action such as this, the burden is fairly and squarely on the Plaintiff to prove title to the land in the manner pleaded in the plaint.",SRC001-s2,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca-dcf-859-1998.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca-dcf-859-1998.pdf,,COA,1998,A party cannot claim prescriptive rights to a land if the date of commencement of adverse possession is specified to thwart the other party's attempt to claim the land on prescriptive possession.,"Without offering an explanation for the basis upon which the Plaintiffs say the Defendant commenced adverse possession of the land, the specific date mentioned in the plaint is misleading and intended to thwart any attempt on the part of the Defendant to claim the land on prescriptive possession, which he would have been entitled to do had the date of his commencement of adverse possession been a mere eight and a half months prior to that claimed by the Plaintiffs.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-PHC-124-22-Judgment-Final.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-PHC-124-22-Judgment-Final.pdf,,COA,,The lessor retains proprietary rights in the leased equipment and is entitled to recover possession upon default.,"Under the Leasing Act, a finance lease is an arrangement whereby possession and use of equipment are transferred to the lessee while ownership remains vested in the lessor.",SRC001-s15,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-PHC-126-20-Judgment-Final.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-PHC-126-20-Judgment-Final.pdf,,COA,,The pendency of civil proceedings does not constitute a legal bar to the institution or continuation of proceedings under Section 66.,"At its highest, the existence of such proceedings may be considered as surrounding circumstance, however, it does not undermine the Magistrate’s duty to inquire into the existence of a dispute affecting land and the likelihood of a breach of the peace, and to make such provisional orders as are necessary in that regard.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-PHC-44-18-final.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-PHC-44-18-final.pdf,,COA,,"Revisionary intervention is available where a subordinate court proceeds upon a misconception of its statutory powers, grants relief beyond the scope of its jurisdiction, fails to identify the jurisdictional facts required by statute, or reaches a determination unsupported by the material before it.","Revision is not intended to function as a further appeal. However, where a subordinate court proceeds upon a misconception of its statutory powers, grants relief beyond the scope of its jurisdiction, fails to identify the jurisdictional facts required by statute, or reaches a determination unsupported by the material before it, revisionary intervention becomes available.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-Writ-742-24-final.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-Writ-742-24-final.pdf,,COA,,The Board shall consider all circumstances of the case when deciding whether a transfer of immovable property is in reality a mortgage.,"21A. (1) In any proceedings under this Ordinance in regard to an application relating to a transfer or conditional transfer of immovable property, the Board shall, notwithstanding anything to the contrary in section 2 of the Prevention of Frauds Ordinance (Chapter 70), or sections 91 and 92 of the Evidence Ordinance (Chapter 14) and for the purpose of deciding whether or not such transfer or conditional transfer is in reality a mortgage, take into consideration all the circumstances of the case and in particular the following matters:-",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA-WRT-0385-23-1.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA-WRT-0385-23-1.pdf,,COA,,A court may consider the disparity between the consideration on the face of the deed and the market value of the property as evidence that the transferor did not intend to pass the beneficial interest in the property.,"The gross disparity between the consideration on the face of the deed ( Rs. 17,000) and the market value of the property ( Rs 70,000- 80,000)",SRC004-s83,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-CA.PHC_.-0046.2019-27.09.2024.pdf-1,courts-coa-CA.PHC_.-0046.2019-27.09.2024.pdf,,COA,2024,The Chairman of the LRC qualifies as a competent authority under the Act.,"Based on the analysis above, I conclude that the Chairman of the LRC qualifies as a competent authority under the Act.",SRC001-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca.phc_.65.03.final_.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca.phc_.65.03.final_.pdf,,COA,,"A Primary Court Judge can determine who is entitled to a right in or respecting the land, and make an order declaring that person's entitlement until they are deprived of it by a competent court.","An order under this subsection may declare that any person specified therein shall be entitled to any such right in or respecting the land or in any part of the land as may be specified in the order until such person is deprived of such right by virtue of an order or decree of a competent court, and prohibit all disturbance or interference with the exercise of such right by such party other than under the authority of an order or decree as aforesaid.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_1160_00.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_1160_00.pdf,,COA,,A person who asserts the existence of a fact has the burden of proof.,lithe burden of proof in a suit or proceeding lies on that person who would fail if no evidence at all were given on either side,SRC029-s102,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_1295_1998.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_1295_1998.pdf,,COA,1998,"A tenant cultivator's rights are decided by the prevalent law, such as the Paddy Lands Act or Agricultural Lands Law, and cannot be changed without strong evidence to satisfy the requirements of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.","When a person enters a property in a particular capacity i. e tenant cultivator, his rights would be decided on the prevalent law namely the Paddy Lands Act or Agricultural Lands Law depending on the relevant period.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_242_99.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_242_99.pdf,,COA,,The burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on the defendant to establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"According to section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance such a possession must be undisturbed, uninterrupted, adverse to or independent of that of the former possessor and should have lasted for at least ten years before he could transform such possession into prescriptive title.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_476_1999.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_476_1999.pdf,,COA,1999,"The property which is the subject of the act of spoliation must be restored to the person from whom it was taken, irrespective of the question as to who is in law entitled to be in possession of such property.","Spoliatus ante omnia restituendus est., which means",SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
