scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_gampaha_1133_00.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_gampaha_1133_00.pdf,,COA,,"An oral agreement affecting immovable property is not enforceable in law unless it is in writing and signed by the party making the same, or by some person lawfully authorized by him or her, in the presence of a licensed notary public and two or more witnesses present at the same time.","In terms of Section 2 of the Prevention of Fraud Ordinance no contract or agreement affecting land or other immovable property shall be of force or avail in law unless the same shall be in writing and signed by the party making the same, or by some person lawfully authorized by him or her in the presence of a licensed notary public and two or more witnesses present at the same time, and unless the execution of such writing, deed, or instrument be duly attested by such notary and witnesses.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_homagama_1113_98.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_homagama_1113_98.pdf,,COA,,"A person can claim prescriptive title to land if they have been in possession of the land for a period of over ten years, adverse to the rights of the original owner.","In the circumstances, it is abundantly clear that the respondent had been in possession of the land claimed by the plaintiff for a period of well over ten years prior to the filing of this action on 03.05.1991, adverse to the rights of the plaintiff which is the criteria applicable to claim prescriptive title in terms of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
