scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_kuliyapitiya_293_00.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_kuliyapitiya_293_00.pdf,,COA,,A claim for recovery of property belonging to a temple is not barred by prescription.,"In the case of any claim for the recovery of any property, movable or immovable, belonging or alleged to belong to any temple, or for the assertion of title to any such property, the claim shall not be held to be barred or prejudiced by any provision of the Prescription Ordinance:",SRC071-s34,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-coa-ca_dc_mathugama_367_1998.pdf-1,courts-coa-ca_dc_mathugama_367_1998.pdf,,COA,1998,The burden of proof rests squarely and fairly on the defendant to establish a starting point for their acquisition of prescriptive rights.,"In a case of this nature once the Plaintiff-Respondent proves title by way of a transfer deed and seek to establish paper title, the burden is on the Defendant to establish superior title by prescriptive rights. i.e to satisfy the requirements of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
