scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_69_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_69_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,A licensee cannot claim prescriptive title to a property without an overt act demonstrating adverse possession.,"A person who is let into occupation of property as a tenant or as a licensee must be deemed to continue to occupy on the footing on which he was admitted, until by some overt act he manifests his intention of occupying in another capacity.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2010.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2010.pdf,,SC,2010,An appellate court should be slow to interfere with a trial judge's decision on facts and circumstances of a case.,"Hence, they become the best judges as to the facts of the case and His Lordship with approval referred to the observation made by Justice G.P.S.de Silva (as he then was) in the case of Alwis v. Piyasena Fernando 1993 1 SLR 111",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_73_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A co-owner may sue another co-owner of an adjoining land for the definition of boundaries without the participation of other co-owners.,"It is a well-established principle of law that a co-owner could sue a trespasser without the participation of other co-owners. (Hevawitrane v. Dangan Rubber Co., Ltd. 17 NLR 49; Arnolisa et al v. Dissan et al 4 NLR 163; Meera Lebbe Cassi Lebbe Marikkar v. Kalavilage Baba (1885) 7 S.C.C.) If a co-owner could sue a trespasser, in the same way a co-owner should be able to be sued without participation of other co-owners.",SRC029-s115,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_74_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_74_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,A subsequent purchase of title by a party claiming prescription to the same land does not negate the prescriptive title acquired by such party to such land.,"any subsequent purchase of title by a party claiming prescription to the same land, does not negate the prescriptive title acquired by such party to such land.",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_75_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_75_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A party does not need to show any other right to use a road if it is part of the subject matter in a previous partition action.,"Hence, the Plaintiff has every right to use this road as part of the subject matter in that partition action.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_77_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_77_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A party's prescriptive title to a defined portion of land can be established by showing possession of that portion for a period of over 60 years.,"the Defendant-Appellant contends inter alia, that she and her predecessors in title possessed the unallotted Lot No. 3 in extent of 2R and 8.20P, depicted in Preliminary Plan No. 1175/P, dated 30/07/1998, made by A.C.P. Gunasena, Licensed Surveyor, for a period of over 60 years and thereby acquired prescriptive title to the said Lot No.3 in the said Plan No. 1175/P.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_78_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_78_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,A mere statement of a witness of occupying a land for long years would not suffice to satisfy the requirements of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,A mere statement of a witness of occupying a land for long years would not suffice to satisfy the requirements of Section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_7_2004.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_7_2004.pdf,,SC,2004,At least one of the two attesting witnesses should also be called to prove the due execution of a deed when the execution is challenged and at least one of the attesting witnesses is alive.,"where the execution of a deed is challenged on the ground that it had been signed before it was written, then, where at least one of the two attesting witnesses is alive, the evidence of the notary alone, even where he knew the executant, is not sufficient; at least one of the two attesting witnesses should also be called.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_7_2020.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_7_2020.pdf,,SC,2020,"A person can acquire prescriptive title to land by possessing it for a long time, even if they do not claim it as part of a larger landholding.",There is no doubt that the 4th Defendant has prescribed to Lots D and E by a title adverse to or independent of that of the Plaintiff and the 1st to 3rd Defendants as required by section 3 of the Prescription Ordinance.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_80_2022.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_80_2022.pdf,,SC,2022,Adverse possession commences from the moment the defendant intends to possess the land of the plaintiff as if he were the owner of it.,"There must be a corporeal occupation of land attended with a manifest intention to hold and continue it and when the intent plainly is to hold the land against the claim of all other persons, the possession is hostile or adverse to the rights of the true owner",SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_82_2021.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_82_2021.pdf,,SC,2021,The burden of proof lies on the person who would fail if no evidence at all were given on either side.,Section 102 – “The burden of proof in a suit or proceeding lies on that person who would fail if no evidence at all were given on either side.”,SRC029-s102,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_83_2013.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_83_2013.pdf,,SC,2013,Dispossession may be by force or by not allowing the possessor to use at his discretion what he possesses.,"Dispossession may be by force or by not allowing the possessor to use at his discretion what he possesses whether it is done by sowing, or ploughing or by building or repairing something or by doing anything at all by which they do not leave the free possession of the person who was dispossessed.",SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_92_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_92_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A tenant cannot deny the landlord's title to the property during the continuance of the tenancy.,"No tenant of immovable property, or person claiming through such tenant, shall during the continuance of the tenancy, be permitted to deny that the landlord of such tenant had, at the beginning of the tenancy, a title to such immovable property;",SRC029-s116,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_93_2015.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_93_2015.pdf,,SC,2015,A party is not entitled to plead legal or paper title and prescriptive title together in the same action.,a party is not entitled to plead legal or paper title and prescriptive title together in the same action,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_93_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_93_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,"A party cannot invoke the extraordinary jurisdiction of revision as of right, and must explain in the petition why they did not exercise their right of appeal.","When a right of appeal is available against a Judgment or an Order, a party seeking to come before Court by way of revision shall explain in the petition why he did not exercise his right of appeal.",SRC071-s3,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_94_2013-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_94_2013-1.pdf,,SC,2013,A possessory action under Section 4 of the Prescription Ordinance does not apply when the plaintiff's case is based on the terms and conditions of a lease agreement.,"Therefore, merely because the reliefs sought in the plaint are to restore him in possession, it cannot change the character and the scope of the plaint particularly when arguing an appeal.",SRC071-s4,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
