scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2016.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2016.pdf,,SC,2016,A notary who attests a document in terms of Prevention of Frauds Ordinance is generally competent to testify under section 68 of the Evidence Ordinance.,A notary who attests a deed is an attesting witness within the meaning of that expression in section 68 and section 69 of the Evidence Ordinance.,SRC001-s68,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2017.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_appeal_9_2017.pdf,,SC,2017,"Once paper title becomes undisputed, the burden shifts to the defendants to show that they have independent rights in the form of prescription as claimed by them.",Once paper title became undisputed the burden shifted to the defendants to show that they had independent rights in the form of prescription as claimed by them.,SRC071-s3,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_16_2007.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_16_2007.pdf,,SC,2007,The President's belief in the necessity or expediency of an emergency regulation is deemed conclusive of its validity.,"As established in the case of Yasapala, the President's belief in the necessity or expediency of an emergency regulation is deemed conclusive of its validity, as he is not bound as a matter of law to disclose the reasons for the proclamation.",SRC001-s2,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_19_2013-1.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_19_2013-1.pdf,,SC,2013,A party may lead evidence aliunde to controvert the consideration stated in a deed.,"By reason of proviso (1) to Section 92 of the Evidence Ordinance, a party is not per se precluded from leading evidence aliunde to controvert the consideration stated in a deed.",SRC029-s92,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_1_2014.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_1_2014.pdf,,SC,2014,A party cannot recover a debt if the amount due has prescribed under the Prescription Ordinance.,"Focusing his attention on the sums of money purportedly due to the Plaintiff from the Defendant on account of the ‘running account’ (“P5” to “P23”), the learned Judge has noted that the amount due per each invoice had become prescribed (as per section 8 of the Prescription Ordinance) as a result of action not having been filed within 1 year of the last payment due.",SRC071-s8,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_23_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_23_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"The prescription period should be calculated from the date of the alleged cause of action and the institution of the action in the District Court, not from the date on which the case was transferred to the Commercial High Court.","Hence, in computing the prescription period, it should be calculated from the date of the alleged cause of action and the institution of the action in the District Court and not from the date on which the case was transferred to the Commercial High Court.",SRC071-s6,ok-unchecked,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
scripts\case-law-information-extraction\output\v1-backup\rules_high_confidence.csv:draftly-rule-courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_24_2008.pdf-1,courts-sc-sc_chc_appeal_24_2008.pdf,,SC,2008,"A contract of suretyship is in essence a contract by which one person, the surety, agrees to answer for some existing or future liability of another, the principal (or principal debtor), to a third party, the creditor, and by which the surety’s liability is in addition to, and not in substitution for, the liability of the principal.","A contract of suretyship is in essence a contract by which one person, the surety, agrees to answer for some existing or future liability of another, the principal (or principal debtor), to a third party, the creditor, and by which the surety’s liability is in addition to, and not in substitution for, the liability of the principal.",SRC029-s101,none,verbatim,ratio,high,llm-extract,unverified
